/// Admin2QuestionBank — 122 questions (103 single-select, 19 multi-select)
import Foundation

enum Admin2QuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A sales manager reports she cannot see opportunities owned by a rep on her team even though her role is above the rep's role in the hierarchy. The Opportunity OWD is Private. What is the most likely cause?", options: [("A", "Grant Access Using Hierarchies is disabled on the Opportunity object, blocking the role hierarchy from granting the manager access"), ("B", "The rep's profile has \"View All\" revoked, which overrides role hierarchy access for opportunities"), ("C", "A criteria-based sharing rule on Opportunity is restricting access below what OWD already grants"), ("D", "The manager's permission set lacks the \"Read\" object permission on Account")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Grant Access Using Hierarchies can be disabled on custom objects (and Opportunity in some configurations) so that role hierarchy no longer automatically extends record access upward, which explains why the manager loses visibility despite outranking the rep. * B is incorrect: There's no \"View All\" toggle on a profile that overrides hierarchy access for other users — View All is a separate, broader permission on the viewer's own profile, not something the owner's profile can revoke from others. C is incorrect: Sharing rules can only widen access beyond OWD, never narrow it, so a sharing rule cannot be the cause of reduced visibility. D is incorrect: Missing Account read access wouldn't block visibility into the Opportunity record itself.,")
        Question(id: "2", question: "A Deal Desk needs automatic, ongoing Read/Write access to all opportunities with Amount > $1M, regardless of owner. Which TWO configurations would correctly achieve this on a declarative basis? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A criteria-based sharing rule filtering on Amount > $1M, sharing to the Deal Desk public group"), ("B", "An owner-based sharing rule keyed on the Opportunity owner's role"), ("C", "A public group containing the Deal Desk users, referenced by the criteria-based sharing rule instead of individual users"), ("D", "One-time manual sharing applied to all opportunities currently over $1M"), ("E", "Apex managed sharing recalculated nightly by a scheduled batch job")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "* A criteria-based sharing rule automatically evaluates the Amount field on every record regardless of owner, and referencing a public group (rather than individual users) as the share target keeps the rule maintainable as Deal Desk membership changes. * B is incorrect: Owner-based rules key off who owns the record, not a field value like Amount, so they wouldn't catch every qualifying deal regardless of owner. D is incorrect: Manual sharing is a one-time action and won't automatically extend to opportunities created or updated after that point. E is incorrect: A custom Apex batch job is unnecessary engineering overhead when the declarative criteria-based rule already satisfies the requirement continuously.,")
        Question(id: "3", question: "A custom object \"Project__c\" has its OWD set to Controlled by Parent, with the parent being Account (Private OWD). A user has no explicit share on a given Account record. What access does this user have to that Account's related Project__c records?", options: [("A", "Full Read/Write access regardless of Account access, since child records are always independently accessible"), ("B", "The same access level the user has on the parent Account record"), ("C", "Read-only access is always granted, even without Account access"), ("D", "No access, because Controlled by Parent only applies to Master-Detail relationships with rollups enabled")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Controlled by Parent means the child object inherits the sharing/access level a user has on the associated parent record — no Account access means no Project__c access. * A is incorrect: This ignores that Controlled by Parent explicitly ties child access to parent access, not independent access. C is incorrect: There's no guaranteed baseline read access under Controlled by Parent; access is entirely inherited from the parent. D is incorrect: Controlled by Parent is a valid OWD option for master-detail relationships generally and doesn't require rollup summary fields to function.,")
        Question(id: "4", question: "An admin wants a manager to be able to manage users, assign a specific set of profiles and permission sets, and unlock locked-out users — but never be able to change organization-wide defaults or clone profiles. Which feature should be configured?", options: [("A", "System Administrator profile with a restricted permission set"), ("B", "A custom permission set with \"Manage Users\" enabled"), ("C", "Delegated administration"), ("D", "Territory Management with an administrative territory role")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Delegated administration is purpose-built to let a non-admin manage users within specified roles and assign only pre-approved profiles/permission sets, while explicitly blocking OWD changes and profile creation/cloning. * A is incorrect: Any variant of System Administrator access would allow OWD changes, which violates the stated constraint. B is incorrect: \"Manage Users\" alone via a permission set does not restrict which profiles/permission sets can be assigned, nor block OWD changes. D is incorrect: Territory Management governs account/opportunity visibility, not user administration capabilities.,")
        Question(id: "5", question: "A company wants to bundle a base permission set plus two feature-specific permission sets into a single reusable assignment for its support agents, but exclude one specific permission from the bundle only for agents in a trial region. What should the admin do?", options: [("A", "Clone the base permission set three times with different names for each region"), ("B", "Assign all three permission sets individually and rely on profile-level restriction to remove the excluded permission"), ("C", "Convert the permission sets into a custom profile for the trial region"), ("D", "Create a permission set group containing the three permission sets, then mute the specific permission for the trial-region agents")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Permission set groups let you stack permission sets into one assignable unit, and muting within the group is the supported mechanism to suppress a specific permission for a subset of users without altering the underlying permission sets. * A is incorrect: Cloning creates maintenance overhead and doesn't solve the need for one reusable bundle with a targeted exclusion. B is incorrect: Profiles cannot selectively strip permissions that permission sets have granted; profiles set the floor, permission sets only add access. C is incorrect: Creating a new profile duplicates maintenance and abandons the reusable permission-set-group approach entirely.,")
        Question(id: "6", question: "A global company's Salesforce org has SSO configured with an external Identity Provider. Users report they can log in fine when accessing via the SSO portal, but direct login attempts at the standard Salesforce login page fail unexpectedly for new hires. What is the most likely missing configuration?", options: [("A", "My Domain has not been enabled, so SSO redirection at the Salesforce login page cannot be configured for new users"), ("B", "The Identity Provider certificate has expired"), ("C", "The Service Provider's Entity ID does not match the org's instance URL"), ("D", "Login IP Ranges are blocking the new hires' office network")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* My Domain must be enabled before SSO login redirection can be configured on the standard login page; without it, new users hitting the direct login URL won't be routed through the IdP flow at all. * B is incorrect: An expired IdP certificate would break SSO entirely for all users, not just direct-login attempts by new hires. C is incorrect: An Entity ID mismatch would cause an assertion validation error for everyone using SSO, not a direct-login-only symptom. D is incorrect: IP range issues trigger a challenge or block regardless of login method, not something isolated to bypassing SSO redirection.,")
        Question(id: "7", question: "A security team wants any login attempt outside a set of corporate office IP ranges to be entirely blocked, with no exception, for a specific profile. Which setting should be configured?", options: [("A", "Trusted IP Ranges at the org level"), ("B", "Login IP Ranges on the profile"), ("C", "Session Settings idle timeout"), ("D", "Login Hours on the profile")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Login IP Ranges configured on a profile hard-block login attempts from outside the listed ranges — there is no challenge or override, unlike the org-level Trusted IP Ranges setting. * A is incorrect: Trusted IP Ranges at the org level only suppresses identity-verification challenges (like email verification) for users inside the range; it doesn't block access from outside it. C is incorrect: Idle timeout terminates inactive sessions but has nothing to do with restricting login by network location. D is incorrect: Login Hours restricts access by time of day, not by network location.,")
        Question(id: "8", question: "After enabling MFA enforcement in Session Settings, what happens to users who are already logged into an active Salesforce session at the time of the change?", options: [("A", "Their session is immediately terminated and they must re-authenticate with MFA before regaining access"), ("B", "MFA is silently applied in the background without requiring any user action"), ("C", "They can continue their current session unaffected, and MFA is enforced only on their next new login"), ("D", "Their profile is automatically downgraded until they complete MFA setup")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Enforcing MFA via Session Settings applies going forward — active sessions continue uninterrupted, and the MFA challenge is presented the next time the user establishes a new login session. * A is incorrect: Enabling the setting does not forcibly terminate sessions already in progress. B is incorrect: MFA requires active user interaction (a verification method) — it cannot be silently applied without the user registering a factor. D is incorrect: There's no profile downgrade mechanic tied to MFA enforcement; access level is unaffected by MFA status.,")
        Question(id: "9", question: "A company merges Account and Contact information for individual consumers using Person Accounts. A support rep tries to relate one Person Account \"contact\" to a second, unrelated business account as a secondary relationship. What happens?", options: [("A", "It works exactly like a standard Contact, since Person Accounts fully support multiple account relationships"), ("B", "The system automatically creates a duplicate standard Contact to support the second relationship"), ("C", "The relationship is allowed but only visible in reports, not on the record detail page"), ("D", "This isn't possible in the standard model — a Person Account's built-in contact record cannot be related to multiple accounts the way a standard Contact can via Contacts to Multiple Accounts")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Because a Person Account merges the Account and Contact into a single record, its implicit contact cannot participate in the Contacts to Multiple Accounts feature the way an independent standard Contact record can. * A is incorrect: This directly contradicts a known Person Account limitation — it does not behave like a standard Contact for multi-account relationships. B is incorrect: Salesforce does not auto-generate a duplicate Contact record to work around this limitation. C is incorrect: There's no report-only visibility workaround built into the platform for this scenario.,")
        Question(id: "10", question: "An admin is asked to enable Person Accounts for an org that has been running for three years with standard Business Accounts only. What must the admin communicate to stakeholders before proceeding?", options: [("A", "Enabling Person Accounts is irreversible, and it requires at least one active Person Account record type to exist on the Account object first"), ("B", "Person Accounts can be enabled and later disabled if the business changes direction"), ("C", "Enabling Person Accounts automatically converts all existing Business Accounts into Person Accounts"), ("D", "Person Accounts can only be enabled in a sandbox, never in production")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Person Accounts is a one-way, permanent org setting, and Salesforce requires a Person Account record type to already exist (or be created as part of enablement) before the feature is turned on. * B is incorrect: There is no supported path to disable Person Accounts once enabled, making this claim false. C is incorrect: Enabling the feature does not retroactively convert existing Business Account records; it only makes Person Accounts available going forward. D is incorrect: Person Accounts can be enabled in production directly (via a support-assisted process), it isn't restricted to sandbox-only.,")
        Question(id: "11", question: "A public group used in a sharing rule includes Role X plus \"Roles and Subordinates\" for Role Y. A new user is added under a subordinate role beneath Role Y. Which TWO statements about this situation are true? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The new user is automatically included, since \"Roles and Subordinates\" dynamically covers new users placed anywhere below Role Y"), ("B", "No manual re-addition to the public group is required for the sharing rule to extend to this new user"), ("C", "The sharing rule only applies to users who existed in the hierarchy when the rule was created"), ("D", "The admin must manually trigger a full sharing recalculation before this new user gains access"), ("E", "If the new user is later moved to a role entirely outside Role Y's downward hierarchy, they would no longer be covered by this rule")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 4], explanation: "* \"Roles and Subordinates\" membership is fully dynamic: any user positioned anywhere below Role Y in the hierarchy is automatically included, and conversely, moving that user outside Role Y's downward branch would remove them from that membership basis. * B is incorrect: While true that no manual re-addition is needed, this doesn't fully capture the mechanism the way A and E together do, so it's not one of the two best-supported statements to select here relative to the others. C is incorrect: The rule isn't frozen to a point-in-time snapshot of hierarchy membership; it evaluates dynamically, so this is false. D is incorrect: No manual recalculation trigger is required for this hierarchy-based dynamic membership to take effect, so this is false.,")
        Question(id: "12", question: "A territory model is currently in Planning state while the admin builds out territory hierarchy and assignment rules. The admin tries to run assignment rules to test account assignment. What happens?", options: [("A", "Assignment rules can be run and tested freely in Planning state, since Planning is meant for build-and-test activity"), ("B", "Assignment rules cannot run against real account data until the model is activated, since only an Active model supports live assignment and user association"), ("C", "Assignment rules run but only assign a random 10% sample of matching accounts for validation purposes"), ("D", "Planning state automatically converts to Active once any assignment rule is created")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* A territory model must be in Active state before its assignment rules can run against live account data and before territories can be associated with users — Planning is purely a design-time state. * A is incorrect: This is the opposite of how territory model states work; live rule execution is gated to Active state. C is incorrect: There's no sampling feature tied to Planning-state execution. D is incorrect: State transitions are a manual admin action, not an automatic side effect of rule creation.,")
        Question(id: "13", question: "A record's OWD is Private, the owner's role sits several levels below the viewing user's role in the hierarchy, Grant Access Using Hierarchies is enabled for the object, and a criteria-based sharing rule also grants access to a public group the viewer belongs to. Which TWO statements correctly describe how the viewer's final access is determined? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The viewer receives the most permissive access level among all applicable mechanisms, since sharing mechanisms are additive rather than mutually exclusive"), ("B", "Sharing rules always take precedence over role hierarchy access when levels differ"), ("C", "Role hierarchy access always overrides an independently valid sharing rule grant"), ("D", "If the criteria-based rule grants Read/Write and hierarchy only grants Read, the viewer ends up with Read/Write"), ("E", "Conflicting access levels between mechanisms never result in the record being hidden from the viewer entirely")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "* Multiple sharing mechanisms apply cumulatively, and the viewer always ends up with the single most permissive access level granted by any applicable mechanism — illustrated concretely by B, where Read/Write from the sharing rule wins over the lesser Read from hierarchy. * B is incorrect: There is no fixed precedence making sharing rules automatically outrank hierarchy access; both simply contribute additively. C is incorrect: Hierarchy access doesn't override or invalidate an independently valid sharing rule grant, so this claim reverses the actual behavior. E is incorrect: While true as a standalone fact, it's not one of the two most directly responsive statements to how access is actually calculated in this scenario, compared to A and D.,")
        Question(id: "14", question: "A report folder needs to be visible to an entire division spanning multiple roles and their subordinates, without listing every individual role separately. What is the most maintainable sharing configuration?", options: [("A", "Share the folder with each individual role and its subordinates one at a time"), ("B", "Share the folder with every individual user by name"), ("C", "Share the folder with a public group that includes \"Roles and Subordinates\" for the division's top role"), ("D", "Set the folder to \"This folder is accessible by all users\" org-wide")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Using a public group that captures \"Roles and Subordinates\" for the division's top-level role covers the whole division dynamically as membership changes, which is exactly the maintainable pattern for folder sharing. * A is incorrect: Listing every role individually recreates the exact maintenance burden the scenario is trying to avoid. B is incorrect: Naming individual users breaks the moment org structure changes and doesn't scale. D is incorrect: Making it fully public exceeds the stated scope of \"the division\" and over-shares to unrelated users.,")
        Question(id: "15", question: "A dashboard folder is shared with the Sales public group at the \"Viewer\" access level. A sales manager in that group tries to modify a dashboard component's filter and save the change back to the shared folder. What happens?", options: [("A", "The manager can save changes, because Viewer access allows editing existing dashboards"), ("B", "The manager can save changes only if they are also the dashboard's original creator"), ("C", "Viewer access allows saving a copy but never overwriting the original in the shared folder"), ("D", "The manager cannot save changes to the folder; Viewer only allows viewing dashboards already in the folder, and Editor or Manager access is required to save changes back")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Viewer access on a dashboard folder is read-only for that folder's contents — modifying and re-saving a dashboard into the folder requires Editor (or Manager) access level. * A is incorrect: This inverts the actual behavior — Viewer explicitly excludes save/edit rights on the folder's contents. B is incorrect: Folder access level, not original authorship, governs whether a save can succeed. C is incorrect: There's no \"save as copy only\" carve-out tied to Viewer-level folder access.,")
        Question(id: "16", question: "A junior admin proposes removing a user's \"Delete\" object permission via a permission set to prevent them from deleting Case records, even though their profile grants Delete on Case. Will this work?", options: [("A", "No — permission sets can only add permissions on top of the profile; they cannot revoke a permission the profile already grants"), ("B", "Yes, permission sets can remove any permission the profile grants"), ("C", "Yes, but only for custom objects, not standard objects like Case"), ("D", "No, because Delete permission cannot be assigned or removed through permission sets at all")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Permission sets are strictly additive — they extend what a profile grants but have no mechanism to subtract or revoke a permission the assigned profile already provides. * B is incorrect: This misstates the fundamental additive-only nature of permission sets. C is incorrect: The additive-only limitation applies uniformly to standard and custom objects alike. D is incorrect: Delete is a standard object permission that can absolutely be granted via permission sets — the issue here is revocation, not assignment.,")
        Question(id: "17", question: "An admin needs Field-Level Security to fully prevent a group of users from ever seeing a sensitive field's value, including through API access and integrations, not just on the page layout. What should the admin rely on?", options: [("A", "Removing the field from all page layouts assigned to those users' profiles"), ("B", "Setting Field-Level Security to hidden for those profiles/permission sets"), ("C", "Making the field read-only on the Lightning page component"), ("D", "Using a validation rule to blank out the field for those users")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Field-Level Security is the authoritative security layer enforced everywhere, including the API, reports, and integrations — layout removal alone only hides a field from that specific UI view, not from other access paths. * A is incorrect: Removing a field from a page layout is a UI convenience only; the field remains fully readable via API, reports, and other layouts. C is incorrect: A read-only component setting still displays the field's value; it doesn't prevent visibility or API access. D is incorrect: A validation rule fires on save and cannot retroactively block read access to existing field data.,")
        Question(id: "18", question: "A company enforces MFA org-wide via a Permission Set assigned to all internal users, but a small group of API-only integration users cannot use MFA factors. What should the admin do?", options: [("A", "Enable MFA anyway and have the integration accounts share a single verified phone number"), ("B", "Add the integration users to Trusted IP Ranges so MFA is bypassed org-wide"), ("C", "Exclude the integration users from the MFA-enforcing permission set and rely on a Connected App policy or named credential-based authentication instead"), ("D", "Lower the org's password policy for those accounts to compensate")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* API-only integration users authenticate through OAuth/Connected App flows rather than interactive login, so they should be excluded from the interactive MFA permission set and secured instead through Connected App-level controls like IP restrictions and named credentials. * A is incorrect: Sharing a phone number across accounts undermines the individual-factor security model MFA is meant to enforce and isn't a supported pattern. B is incorrect: Trusted IP Ranges only suppresses identity-verification challenges for interactive logins; it doesn't function as a global MFA bypass mechanism for API access. D is incorrect: Weakening password policy increases risk and does not address the underlying MFA-factor limitation for API users.,")
        Question(id: "19", question: "An admin configures a Connected App and wants every user in the org to use it without each individually approving OAuth access on first use. What should be configured?", options: [("A", "Ask every user to click \"Allow\" the first time they use the app"), ("B", "Disable OAuth entirely and use only session-based login"), ("C", "Add the Connected App to a public group"), ("D", "Set the Connected App's OAuth policy to \"Admin approved users are pre-authorized\" and assign it via profile or permission set")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Pre-authorizing a Connected App via profiles or permission sets lets an admin grant org-wide access up front, skipping the individual per-user consent screen that would otherwise appear. * A is incorrect: Requiring individual approval is exactly the friction the admin is trying to eliminate, not a configuration solution. B is incorrect: Disabling OAuth would break the Connected App's ability to authenticate at all, not streamline it. C is incorrect: Connected App access is governed by profile/permission set assignment, not public group membership.,")
        Question(id: "20", question: "A company's OWD for Case is Private. A support manager wants their team's queue-based case access to also let account executives with no case-specific sharing view cases tied to their named accounts. What is the most declarative solution?", options: [("A", "A criteria-based sharing rule on Case using the related Account's Owner field, sharing to the account executives' role"), ("B", "A criteria-based sharing rule based on Case owner's queue membership"), ("C", "An owner-based sharing rule sharing cases owned by the support queue with the account executives' role"), ("D", "Manually sharing each case with the relevant account executive")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* A criteria-based sharing rule can key off a field like the related Account's Owner to automatically extend visibility to the AE tied to that account, without requiring manual per-case sharing. * B is incorrect: Queue membership of the case owner isn't the criterion that ties the case to the AE — the AE relationship is via the Account, not the queue. C is incorrect: Owner-based rules only look at record ownership, not the related Account's owner, so this wouldn't connect cases to the right AE. D is incorrect: Manual sharing doesn't scale and requires ongoing admin effort for every new case.,")
        Question(id: "21", question: "A junior admin wants to grant a temp contractor full CRUD access to a single custom object for 30 days only, without touching the contractor's assigned profile. What is the best approach?", options: [("A", "Temporarily change the contractor's profile to System Administrator"), ("B", "Assign a permission set granting the needed object permissions, then remove the permission set assignment after 30 days"), ("C", "Set the object's OWD to Public Read/Write for the duration"), ("D", "Grant access via a 30-day expiring role hierarchy change")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* A permission set is the right additive, easily reversible tool for granting scoped, temporary object access without altering the contractor's base profile or affecting other users. * A is incorrect: Changing the profile to System Administrator grants far more than the single object's CRUD access and violates least-privilege practice. C is incorrect: Changing OWD to Public Read/Write affects every user in the org, not just the one contractor. D is incorrect: Role hierarchy doesn't grant object-level CRUD by itself and isn't designed for time-boxed individual access grants.,")
        Question(id: "22", question: "An org uses Territory Management 2.0. An account is manually assigned to Territory A, but the account also matches the filter-based assignment rule criteria for Territory B. What happens to the account's territory assignment?", options: [("A", "The manual assignment to Territory A is overwritten by the rule-based assignment to Territory B on the next rule run"), ("B", "The account can only ever have one territory, and the manual assignment always blocks any rule-based assignment attempt"), ("C", "The account is assigned to both Territory A and Territory B, since manual and rule-based assignments can coexist"), ("D", "The system throws an error requiring the admin to resolve the conflict before saving")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Territory Management 2.0 supports multiple simultaneous territory associations on an account — manual assignments and rule-based (filter) assignments are not mutually exclusive and can both apply. * A is incorrect: Rule-based assignment runs don't overwrite or remove manual assignments; they add matching accounts rather than replacing existing ones. B is incorrect: An account isn't restricted to a single territory under Territory Management 2.0, so this description misrepresents the model. D is incorrect: There's no validation error blocking coexistence of manual and rule-based territory assignments on the same account.,")
        Question(id: "23", question: "A nonprofit's OWD for Contact is Private. Field-Level Security hides the \"Donation_Amount__c\" field from the Volunteer Coordinator profile, but a sharing rule grants that profile Read/Write access to all Contact records owned by the Development team. Can Volunteer Coordinators see Donation_Amount__c values via a report?", options: [("A", "Yes, because record-level sharing rule access always overrides Field-Level Security in reports"), ("B", "Yes, but only in list views, not in reports"), ("C", "No, but only because the sharing rule grants Read/Write instead of Read-only access"), ("D", "No, because Field-Level Security blocks the field everywhere, including in reports, regardless of record-level access granted by the sharing rule")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Field-Level Security is enforced independently of record-level sharing — even with full record access via the sharing rule, a field hidden by FLS remains invisible in reports, list views, and the API alike. * A is incorrect: Record-level access from a sharing rule has no power to override a field being hidden by FLS; these are separate security layers. B is incorrect: FLS applies uniformly across list views and reports — there's no report-specific exception. C is incorrect: The access level (Read vs. Read/Write) granted by the sharing rule is irrelevant to whether FLS hides the field.,")
        Question(id: "24", question: "A delegated admin manages users under Role X and its subordinates. A reorganization moves Role X to report under a different top-level role, but keeps its own subordinate structure intact. What happens to the delegated admin's management scope?", options: [("A", "The delegated admin's scope automatically continues to cover Role X and its subordinates, since delegation is defined relative to the role, not its position in the hierarchy"), ("B", "The delegated admin loses all access because the delegation group must be manually recreated after any hierarchy change"), ("C", "The delegated admin gains access to the new parent role's other subordinate branches as well"), ("D", "The reorganization is blocked until the delegated admin assignment is removed")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Delegated administration scope is tied to the specific roles designated in the delegation group (Role X and its subordinates), so moving Role X elsewhere in the hierarchy doesn't change which roles the delegated admin can manage. * B is incorrect: There's no requirement to manually recreate the delegation group after an unrelated hierarchy reorganization. C is incorrect: Scope is defined by the specific roles listed, not by proximity in the new hierarchy position, so sibling branches aren't automatically included. D is incorrect: Salesforce does not block role hierarchy changes because a delegated admin group references one of the roles being moved. --- #,")
        Question(id: "25", question: "A company needs a many-to-many relationship between Projects and Consultants, where deleting a Project should not delete Consultant records but should remove the assignment records. Which TWO design elements are required to achieve this? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A junction object with a master-detail relationship to Project"), ("B", "A single lookup field on Consultant pointing to Project"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field on Project referencing Consultant directly"), ("D", "A hierarchical relationship field on Consultant"), ("E", "A junction object with a master-detail relationship to Consultant")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 4], explanation: "* A true many-to-many junction object needs master-detail relationships to both parent objects — one to Project and one to Consultant — so cascade delete on the Project side removes only the junction assignment records, not the Consultant records themselves. * B: A single lookup only supports one Project per Consultant, not a true many-to-many relationship. * C: Roll-up summary fields require a master-detail relationship to already exist and don't themselves create the many-to-many join. * D: Hierarchical relationships are a Lookup subtype for self-referencing records (like a manager field on User), not for many-to-many modeling.,")
        Question(id: "26", question: "An admin wants a roll-up summary field on Account to show the total open Amount across only Opportunities in the \"Negotiation\" stage. Which configuration is required?", options: [("A", "A formula field on Account referencing Opportunity.Amount directly"), ("B", "A roll-up summary field with SUM on Amount and filter criteria limiting to Stage = Negotiation, on a master-detail relationship"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field with COUNT and no filter, since filters are not supported on roll-ups"), ("D", "A cross-object formula field on the Opportunity referencing Account")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Roll-up summary fields support filter criteria to restrict which child records are aggregated, and SUM against Amount with a Stage filter on a master-detail child relationship delivers exactly the described total. * A is incorrect: A simple formula field cannot aggregate across multiple child records; formulas operate on a single record's fields. C is incorrect: Filter criteria on roll-up summary fields are supported and commonly used — this option misstates the capability. D is incorrect: A cross-object formula pulls a single parent field down to the child, the reverse direction of what's needed here.,")
        Question(id: "27", question: "A lookup relationship between Invoice__c and Account has existed for two years with thousands of records. The admin wants to convert it to master-detail to enable roll-up summaries. What must be true first?", options: [("A", "All Invoice__c records must be deleted and recreated after the conversion"), ("B", "The Account object must have fewer than 10,000 records"), ("C", "Every existing Invoice__c record must already have a non-null Account value in the lookup field"), ("D", "A validation rule must be added to enforce the relationship before conversion is allowed")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Converting a lookup to master-detail requires every existing child record to already have a value populated in that lookup field, since master-detail cannot have a null parent reference. * A is incorrect: Deleting and recreating records is unnecessary and would cause unrelated data loss; the conversion works in place once the null-value precondition is met. B is incorrect: There's no record-count ceiling on the parent object tied to this conversion. D is incorrect: Existing validation or default-value rules on the lookup field are actually a blocker to conversion, not a prerequisite to add.,")
        Question(id: "28", question: "A service org wants different picklist values and different page layouts for \"Standard Support\" cases versus \"Premium Support\" cases, and also wants Premium cases to follow a different case status business process. What single feature enables all three?", options: [("A", "Dynamic Forms"), ("B", "Compact Layouts"), ("C", "Two separate custom objects"), ("D", "Record Types")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Record Types are the single mechanism that ties together picklist value sets, page layout assignment, and business process (status/stage) assignment for different variants of the same object. * A is incorrect: Dynamic Forms controls field/section visibility on a Lightning page, but doesn't manage picklist value restriction or business process assignment. B is incorrect: Compact Layouts only control which fields appear in the highlights panel and related lookup cards, not picklists or business processes. C is incorrect: Splitting into two objects abandons a unified Case object and massively overcomplicates reporting and automation.,")
        Question(id: "29", question: "An admin needs to visualize how five custom objects relate to each other and add two new lookup fields across those relationships in one continuous view. Which TWO statements about the right tool are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Object Manager shows multiple objects' relationships simultaneously on one screen"), ("B", "Schema Builder visualizes cross-object relationships across multiple objects on a single canvas"), ("C", "Schema Builder lets an admin create new lookup fields directly within that visual canvas view"), ("D", "Data Loader can visualize object relationships graphically"), ("E", "Process Builder canvas is the recommended tool for visualizing schema relationships")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "* Schema Builder is purpose-built to visualize cross-object relationships across several objects on one canvas, and it lets the admin create new fields (including lookups) directly within that same visual view. * A: Object Manager only shows one object's fields at a time, without the relational, multi-object visualization Schema Builder provides. * D: Data Loader is a data migration tool with no schema visualization capability at all. * E: Process Builder is a retired automation tool with no schema visualization capability.,")
        Question(id: "30", question: "An admin tries to delete a custom field directly from Schema Builder's canvas view. What happens?", options: [("A", "Schema Builder does not support deleting objects or fields; the admin must go to Object Manager to delete the field"), ("B", "The field is deleted immediately, along with its historical data"), ("C", "The field is only hidden from Schema Builder's view, not actually deleted"), ("D", "Deletion is only possible if the field has never been referenced in a formula")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Schema Builder is a creation and visualization tool; it explicitly does not support deleting existing objects or fields, so that action must be performed through Object Manager instead. * B is incorrect: Schema Builder cannot perform this deletion at all, so nothing is deleted through this interface. C is incorrect: There's no \"hide from canvas\" toggle standing in for deletion in Schema Builder. D is incorrect: Formula reference has nothing to do with why Schema Builder lacks a delete capability — it simply isn't built for that action.,")
        Question(id: "31", question: "A Lightning app needs a persistent utility for quick access to a macro tool and a live chat widget across every page in the console, without cluttering the main navigation. What should the admin configure?", options: [("A", "Add both as tabs in the App Launcher"), ("B", "Add both as items in the app's Utility Bar"), ("C", "Add both as Quick Actions on the page layout"), ("D", "Add both as components in the Lightning App Builder App Page")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* The Utility Bar is designed for persistent, always-available tools like macros and chat that stay accessible across every page within a Lightning console app without taking up primary navigation space. * A is incorrect: App Launcher tabs are for navigating between full apps, not for persistent supplementary tools available on every page. C is incorrect: Quick Actions live on record page layouts and aren't a global cross-page utility mechanism. D is incorrect: App Page components are tied to a specific Lightning page, not persistently available across the whole console experience.,")
        Question(id: "32", question: "A Lightning record page needs to show a \"Renewal Risk\" custom component only when the Opportunity's Stage is \"Negotiation\" and the user's profile is \"Sales Manager.\" Dynamic Forms is not enabled on Opportunity in this org's release. What should the admin use?", options: [("A", "A page layout-only field-level restriction"), ("B", "A validation rule that hides the component"), ("C", "Component visibility filters in Lightning App Builder"), ("D", "A separate Visualforce page embedded conditionally via Apex")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Component visibility filters in Lightning App Builder can combine multiple conditions (field value and user permission/profile-based criteria) to conditionally show or hide a component, independent of whether Dynamic Forms is available for that object. * A is incorrect: Page layouts govern fields, not whether a Lightning component renders on the page. B is incorrect: Validation rules fire on save to block or allow data entry; they have no mechanism to control component rendering. D is incorrect: Building custom Visualforce and Apex is unnecessary overengineering when declarative component visibility filters already solve this exact requirement.,")
        Question(id: "33", question: "An org has Dynamic Forms enabled for a custom object \"Warranty_Claim__c.\" The admin wants specific fields to appear only when \"Claim_Type__c\" equals \"Extended.\" What is the correct declarative approach?", options: [("A", "Create two separate page layouts and use Record Types to switch between them"), ("B", "Use a validation rule to blank the fields when Claim_Type__c is not Extended"), ("C", "Use Process Builder to hide the fields at runtime"), ("D", "Add the fields to the Lightning page and apply field-level visibility rules directly within Dynamic Forms")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Dynamic Forms allows field-level (and section-level) visibility rules to be configured directly on the Lightning record page, which is exactly the mechanism intended to replace the multiple-page-layout pattern for conditional field display. * A is incorrect: Building multiple page layouts is the older pattern that Dynamic Forms is meant to replace, and it's unnecessary once Dynamic Forms is enabled. B is incorrect: A validation rule blanking data would destroy field values rather than simply controlling visibility, which isn't the intended behavior. C is incorrect: Process Builder is retired and doesn't control UI-level field rendering on a Lightning page in this way.,")
        Question(id: "34", question: "A support team wants agents to work multiple open cases in separate browser tabs within the same console app, switching quickly without losing context on any one case. Which console feature enables this?", options: [("A", "Workspace tabs"), ("B", "Split view"), ("C", "The Utility Bar"), ("D", "Global Search")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Workspace tabs in the Service Cloud console let agents open multiple records as separate persistent tabs, preserving each record's context so agents can freely switch between several open cases at once. * B is incorrect: Split view is a list-plus-detail layout for browsing a single list's records, not a multi-record tabbed working set. C is incorrect: The Utility Bar hosts persistent tools, not case records themselves. D is incorrect: Global Search helps locate records but doesn't manage multiple concurrently open working records.,")
        Question(id: "35", question: "An admin wants to introduce new Sales Cloud users to a redesigned Opportunity page with a one-time walkthrough the first time they visit it, without requiring a separate license. What should be configured?", options: [("A", "A custom Visualforce onboarding page requiring a new permission set license"), ("B", "In-App Guidance using a Walkthrough prompt type, available to standard Salesforce licensed users"), ("C", "A Trailhead-required badge before the page unlocks"), ("D", "A dashboard component summarizing the changes")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* In-App Guidance walkthroughs are built specifically for this kind of one-time contextual onboarding on a Lightning page and are available to standard Salesforce user licenses without a separate add-on license. * A is incorrect: A custom Visualforce page requiring a new license is unnecessary overhead when standard In-App Guidance already covers the need. C is incorrect: Trailhead badges are external learning content, not an in-app, contextual walkthrough mechanism gating page access. D is incorrect: A dashboard component displays data, not an interactive onboarding walkthrough tied to first-time page visits.,")
        Question(id: "36", question: "Users report a specific Lightning record page loads noticeably slower than others. The admin wants to identify which specific component is causing the slowdown before making changes. What should the admin do first?", options: [("A", "Immediately remove half the components and test by trial and error"), ("B", "Increase the org's API request limit"), ("C", "Use the Analyze button in Lightning App Builder to review the page's performance breakdown by component"), ("D", "Rebuild the entire page as a Visualforce page instead")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* The Analyze button in Lightning App Builder is purpose-built to surface per-component performance data, including slow-loading or error-prone components, which pinpoints the actual bottleneck before any changes are made. * A is incorrect: Trial-and-error removal is inefficient and risks removing components users need, when a direct diagnostic tool already exists. B is incorrect: API request limits govern integration call volume, not Lightning page component rendering speed. D is incorrect: Rebuilding in Visualforce is a drastic, outdated overcorrection with no diagnostic value and abandons the modern Lightning framework.,")
        Question(id: "37", question: "An org has both Business Accounts and Person Accounts enabled. A validation rule referencing \"Contact.MailingCity\" is applied to enforce a required field on standard Contacts. Support reports the rule doesn't seem to apply consistently to Person Account records. Why?", options: [("A", "Validation rules never apply to any Person Account fields under any circumstances"), ("B", "Person Accounts do not support validation rules until Shield Platform Encryption is enabled"), ("C", "The rule only fails because Person Accounts cannot have a MailingCity field at all"), ("D", "Person Account fields are accessed through the merged Account/Contact record structure, so validation logic written purely against a standalone Contact context may not evaluate the way it does for standard Contacts")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Because Person Accounts merge Account and Contact into a single record, field references and automation built with standard, independent Contact assumptions in mind can behave inconsistently and need to be re-validated against the merged Person Account model. * A is incorrect: Person Accounts do support validation rules generally; the issue here is structural inconsistency, not a blanket prohibition. B is incorrect: Shield Platform Encryption is unrelated to whether validation rules can be configured on Person Account fields. C is incorrect: Person Accounts do have address fields like MailingCity available on the merged record; the field itself isn't unavailable.,")
        Question(id: "38", question: "A company wants a report type that pulls fields from Opportunity, its primary Contact Role, and the related Account's Campaign influence data — four objects deep in the relationship chain. Standard report types don't cover this combination. What should the admin build?", options: [("A", "A Custom Report Type spanning the needed objects in a relationship chain, up to the four related objects supported"), ("B", "A joined report combining four separate standard reports"), ("C", "A Big Object designed specifically for this reporting need"), ("D", "A dashboard filter linking four unrelated reports")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Custom Report Types are designed exactly for this scenario — pulling fields across a primary object and up to four related objects in a relationship chain when no standard report type covers the combination. * B is incorrect: Joined reports combine separate report blocks side by side; they don't merge fields across a relationship chain within a single row-level report. C is incorrect: Big Objects are for long-term data archiving and reporting on massive datasets, not for building a standard cross-object field combination. D is incorrect: Dashboard filters narrow existing report data; they don't create new cross-object field relationships.,")
        Question(id: "39", question: "An AppExchange ISV wants to distribute an app where the underlying Apex code is hidden from subscribers, and the ISV can push version upgrades to all installed orgs. Which packaging type is required?", options: [("A", "Unmanaged package"), ("B", "Managed package"), ("C", "Change set"), ("D", "Ant migration tool deployment")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Managed packages use a unique namespace, hide the underlying Apex code from subscribers, and support the ISV pushing version upgrades — exactly the capabilities described. * A is incorrect: Unmanaged packages expose all metadata and code openly and cannot be centrally upgraded by the publisher after installation. C is incorrect: Change sets are for point-to-point metadata migration between orgs an admin controls, not for AppExchange distribution to third-party subscribers. D is incorrect: The Ant migration tool is a metadata deployment utility, not an AppExchange packaging and distribution mechanism.,")
        Question(id: "40", question: "An admin needs to surface data from a legacy on-premise inventory system inside Salesforce in near real time, without replicating or storing that data in Salesforce, and reporting requirements are minimal. Which TWO statements are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A Big Object with a scheduled Apex batch sync provides a live, non-replicated external connection"), ("B", "Salesforce Connect with an OData external object exposes external system data live without storing a copy in Salesforce"), ("C", "Standard Web-to-Lead form submission is designed for surfacing external inventory system data"), ("D", "A nightly Data Loader batch import would replicate and store the data in Salesforce, violating the no-replication requirement"), ("E", "Salesforce Connect external objects generally cannot be reported on the same way native objects can, which matters here given minimal reporting needs")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "* Salesforce Connect with an OData external object matches the near-real-time, no-replication requirement directly, while a nightly Data Loader batch import would do the opposite — replicating and storing a copy of the data in Salesforce. * A: Big Objects store data within Salesforce for archiving; they don't provide a live, non-replicated external connection. * C: Web-to-Lead is a lead capture form mechanism, unrelated to surfacing external inventory system data. * E: While true as a general limitation of external objects, it isn't one of the two most directly responsive statements to the specific tool choice being evaluated here.,")
        Question(id: "41", question: "A Lightning App Builder Home Page needs to show different components depending on whether the logged-in user has the \"Sales Manager\" profile versus the \"Sales Rep\" profile, without creating two separate Home Page assignments. What should be configured?", options: [("A", "Two Home Pages, each assigned via App and Profile assignment"), ("B", "A single Home Page with Dynamic Forms field visibility rules"), ("C", "Component visibility filters based on user profile criteria on a single Home Page"), ("D", "Two separate Lightning apps, one per profile")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Component visibility filters support user-based criteria (like profile), letting a single Home Page conditionally reveal different components per viewer without needing multiple page assignments. * A is incorrect: Creating two separate Home Pages with profile-based assignment is a valid alternative, but the scenario specifically asks to avoid creating two separate assignments — visibility filters is the leaner fit. B is incorrect: Dynamic Forms governs field-level visibility on record pages, not component visibility on a Home Page. D is incorrect: Building two entire separate apps is a large overcorrection for what a single filtered Home Page component can solve.,")
        Question(id: "42", question: "A custom object needs page layout assignment that differs by both Record Type and Profile — Sales Reps using the \"Standard Deal\" record type see one layout, while Sales Managers using the same record type see another. Where is this configured?", options: [("A", "Page Layout Assignment matrix under the object's Record Types, mapping Profile x Record Type combinations to specific layouts"), ("B", "Dynamic Forms field visibility rules"), ("C", "Component visibility filters in Lightning App Builder"), ("D", "A single page layout with conditional field-level security per profile")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* The Page Layout Assignment matrix is exactly where an admin maps combinations of Record Type and Profile to specific page layouts, supporting different layouts for the same record type across different profiles. * B is incorrect: Dynamic Forms governs field-level visibility within a page, not which entire page layout a profile/record-type combination sees. C is incorrect: Component visibility filters apply within Lightning App Builder pages, not to the classic page layout assignment matrix mechanism. D is incorrect: Field-Level Security controls field visibility uniformly per profile; it doesn't switch entire layouts based on record type. --- #,")
        Question(id: "43", question: "A compliance officer needs to know exactly who changed a specific validation rule and when, going back four months. Where should the admin look first?", options: [("A", "Setup Audit Trail, downloaded as CSV if beyond the 180-day UI viewing window"), ("B", "Field History Tracking on the Validation Rule object"), ("C", "Login History"), ("D", "Debug Logs filtered by API version")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Setup Audit Trail records configuration changes like validation rule edits, viewable in the UI for 180 days and downloadable as CSV for up to six months, covering the four-month lookback described. * B is incorrect: Field History Tracking applies to record data field changes on objects, not metadata changes like validation rule edits. C is incorrect: Login History tracks authentication events, not configuration changes to setup metadata. D is incorrect: Debug Logs capture runtime execution traces, not historical setup configuration changes.,")
        Question(id: "44", question: "An admin enables Field History Tracking on the Opportunity object and selects 22 fields to track. What happens?", options: [("A", "All 22 fields are tracked successfully with no issue"), ("B", "Only the first 20 fields can be tracked, since 20 is the maximum number of tracked fields per object"), ("C", "Tracking silently fails for all fields until the count is reduced"), ("D", "The extra 2 fields are tracked but not visible in the UI")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Field History Tracking caps out at 20 tracked fields per object, so the admin will be blocked from selecting more than 20 and must choose which fields matter most. * A is incorrect: This exceeds the documented 20-field limit, which the platform enforces. C is incorrect: The limit blocks additional selections beyond 20; it doesn't silently fail already-configured tracking. D is incorrect: There's no hidden-but-tracked state for fields beyond the limit — the platform simply won't allow more than 20 to be selected.,")
        Question(id: "45", question: "A legal team needs to analyze Opportunity field history data from three years ago, well beyond the 18-month UI retention window. How should the admin retrieve it?", options: [("A", "Query the FieldHistory-related object via the API or Data Loader, since data beyond 18 months isn't shown in the UI but remains queryable"), ("B", "The data is permanently deleted after 18 months and cannot be recovered"), ("C", "Only Salesforce Support can retrieve field history beyond 18 months, and only via a support case"), ("D", "Re-enable Field History Tracking to regenerate the missing historical data")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Field history data older than the 18-month UI display window is still retained and can be retrieved via the API or Data Loader querying the object's history table directly. * B is incorrect: The data isn't deleted at 18 months — that's only the UI display cutoff, not a retention/deletion boundary. C is incorrect: A support case isn't required; standard API/Data Loader access is sufficient to query older field history records. D is incorrect: Re-enabling tracking only affects future changes; it cannot regenerate historical data that already exists and is simply not shown in the UI.,")
        Question(id: "46", question: "A security team wants to investigate a suspicious login attempt from an unusual location that occurred five months ago. Which tool provides this and for how long is the data retained?", options: [("A", "Login History, retained for 6 months"), ("B", "Setup Audit Trail, retained indefinitely"), ("C", "Field History Tracking, retained for 18 months"), ("D", "Health Check, retained for 12 months")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Login History captures the details of login attempts, including location and outcome, and retains that data for 6 months — covering a five-month-old login investigation. * B is incorrect: Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration changes, not individual login attempt details like source location. C is incorrect: Field History Tracking records changes to record field values, unrelated to authentication events. D is incorrect: Health Check is a point-in-time security configuration score, not a historical login event log.,")
        Question(id: "47", question: "An org's Health Check score drops significantly after a review. Which of the following would most likely explain the drop?", options: [("A", "Session timeout was increased to a longer duration and password complexity requirements were relaxed"), ("B", "A new custom object was created with a Private OWD"), ("C", "A new report was added to a public folder"), ("D", "A user's profile was cloned to create a new profile")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Health Check scores organizations against the Salesforce Baseline Standard for security settings like session timeout duration and password complexity — loosening either of these directly and measurably lowers the score. * B is incorrect: OWD settings for a specific custom object aren't part of the Health Check security settings scorecard. C is incorrect: Adding a report to a folder is a content action unrelated to the security configuration settings Health Check evaluates. D is incorrect: Cloning a profile doesn't itself change any of the baseline security settings Health Check measures.,")
        Question(id: "48", question: "A company must comply with a regulation requiring that customer SSNs be encrypted at rest in the database itself, not just masked on the page layout, while remaining usable in exact-match search. Which Salesforce feature satisfies this?", options: [("A", "Classic Encryption"), ("B", "Shield Platform Encryption"), ("C", "Field-Level Security set to hidden"), ("D", "A formula field that masks the last four digits")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Shield Platform Encryption encrypts data at rest in the underlying database while still supporting certain exact-match filtering and search capabilities, meeting both the at-rest encryption and usability requirement. * A is incorrect: Classic Encryption only masks a limited custom text field type in the UI and does not encrypt the value at the database storage layer in the same way. C is incorrect: Field-Level Security controls visibility, not encryption of the stored data itself. D is incorrect: A masking formula field only changes displayed output; the underlying stored value remains unencrypted.,")
        Question(id: "49", question: "A compliance audit requires a sandbox for penetration testing that contains a complete, exact replica of all production data, including every custom object's full record set. Which TWO statements about the correct sandbox type are true? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Partial Copy replicates all production data completely, just like Full Copy"), ("B", "Full Copy is the only sandbox type that replicates all production data completely"), ("C", "Developer Pro sandboxes include full production data replication by default"), ("D", "Developer sandboxes have no refresh interval restriction whatsoever"), ("E", "Full Copy carries a minimum 29-day refresh interval")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "* Full Copy is uniquely the only sandbox type replicating a complete production data set, and that comprehensive replication is exactly why it carries the platform's longest refresh interval restriction, a 29-day minimum. * A: Partial Copy only includes a sampled subset of data via a sandbox template, not a complete replica like Full Copy. * C: Developer Pro includes no production data replication by default, unlike Full Copy. * D: Developer sandboxes do carry their own refresh interval restriction (a 1-day minimum), so this claim is false.,")
        Question(id: "50", question: "A company refreshes a Partial Copy sandbox for UAT testing but is alarmed to see real customer credit card numbers appear in test data visible to a wide QA team. What should the admin have configured beforehand?", options: [("A", "A sandbox template scoped to exclude sensitive objects, combined with a data masking tool applied to the sandbox"), ("B", "A tighter Login IP Range on the sandbox"), ("C", "A stricter OWD on the production Account object"), ("D", "Field-Level Security hiding the field only in production")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Sandbox data masking tools (and carefully scoped sandbox templates) exist specifically to prevent sensitive production data like credit card numbers from surfacing unmasked in lower environments such as Partial Copy sandboxes. * B is incorrect: IP restrictions control who can log in, not what sensitive data is visible once logged in. C is incorrect: Production OWD settings don't get carried over to control what test data looks like post-refresh in a way that masks sensitive values. D is incorrect: Field-Level Security applied only in production has no effect on how that data appears in the refreshed sandbox copy.,")
        Question(id: "51", question: "A Critical Update is scheduled to auto-activate in 30 days. The admin has not yet tested it. What is the recommended course of action?", options: [("A", "Ignore it, since Critical Updates only affect sandbox orgs"), ("B", "Deactivate all other Critical Updates to compensate for the risk"), ("C", "Permanently opt out of the update by contacting Salesforce Support"), ("D", "Enable the Critical Update in a sandbox first to test for impact, then decide whether to activate early or let it auto-activate in production after validation")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* The recommended and available practice is to activate and test Critical Updates in a sandbox before their auto-activation date, so any impact is understood and addressed ahead of the mandatory production activation. * A is incorrect: Critical Updates apply to production orgs as well and will auto-activate there regardless of sandbox-only assumptions. B is incorrect: Deactivating unrelated Critical Updates does nothing to mitigate the risk of the specific update in question. C is incorrect: Most Critical Updates cannot be permanently opted out of indefinitely — they are designed to eventually auto-activate.,")
        Question(id: "52", question: "Reviewing Health Check, an admin sees that \"Password complexity requirement\" and \"Session timeout value\" are both flagged as high-risk deviations from baseline. What is the most effective way to improve the score?", options: [("A", "Bring both settings in line with the Salesforce Baseline Standard recommendations directly in Session Settings and Password Policies"), ("B", "Ignore Health Check since it only affects sandbox orgs"), ("C", "Only fix the setting with the larger point deduction and leave the other as-is"), ("D", "Ask users to individually strengthen their own passwords without changing org policy")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Directly updating the flagged settings to match Salesforce's Baseline Standard is the direct, effective way to close the specific gaps Health Check identified and raise the score. * B is incorrect: Health Check evaluates production org security settings and is meant to directly inform production hardening, not just sandbox review. C is incorrect: Leaving a flagged high-risk setting unaddressed continues to suppress the score and leaves a real security gap. D is incorrect: Individual user behavior can't substitute for an org-wide policy setting that Health Check is specifically measuring. --- #,")
        Question(id: "53", question: "A rep tries to add a new product to an Opportunity's line items, but the product doesn't appear in the product picker. What is the most likely cause?", options: [("A", "The Opportunity Stage is not yet \"Closed Won\""), ("B", "The product has not been added to the Standard Price Book or an active custom price book"), ("C", "The rep's role is below the product owner's role in the hierarchy"), ("D", "The product record's OWD is set to Private")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* A product must be added to at least one active price book before it can be selected as an Opportunity line item; without that step, it simply won't appear in the picker. * A is incorrect: Opportunity Stage has no bearing on whether a product is selectable as a line item. C is incorrect: Products aren't governed by owner-based role hierarchy visibility in the way records are; the price book association is the gating factor. D is incorrect: Product OWD affects record-level visibility of the Product2 record, not whether it's been added to a price book for selection.,")
        Question(id: "54", question: "A manufacturer sells equipment with an installation schedule spread over 6 months and wants both the delivery quantities per month and the associated revenue recognized per month tracked against the same Opportunity Product. Which schedule type should be enabled?", options: [("A", "Quantity schedule only"), ("B", "Revenue schedule only"), ("C", "A combined quantity and revenue schedule"), ("D", "A custom object replicating schedule data manually")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* A combined schedule tracks both quantity and revenue installments against the same Opportunity Product line item, which matches the need to track delivery quantities and recognized revenue together over time. * A: A quantity-only schedule wouldn't capture the revenue recognition tracking the manufacturer also needs. * B: A revenue-only schedule wouldn't capture the delivery quantity tracking the manufacturer also needs. * D: A custom object replicating this manually is unnecessary engineering effort when the native combined schedule type already meets both requirements together.,")
        Question(id: "55", question: "A sales rep creates a Quote from an Opportunity and syncs it. Later, the rep creates a second Quote on the same Opportunity and syncs that one instead. What happens to the first synced Quote?", options: [("A", "Both quotes remain synced simultaneously"), ("B", "The first Quote is deleted permanently"), ("C", "The Opportunity is locked from further quote syncing until the first Quote is manually deleted"), ("D", "The first Quote is automatically unsynced, since only one quote can be synced to an Opportunity at a time")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Only one Quote can be actively synced to an Opportunity at any time — syncing a new Quote automatically unsyncs the previous one rather than allowing two simultaneous syncs. * A is incorrect: Simultaneous dual-sync isn't supported; the platform enforces a single synced Quote per Opportunity. B is incorrect: The previously synced Quote record itself isn't deleted, just unsynced — it still exists as a Quote record. C is incorrect: There's no manual-deletion requirement blocking the new sync; the platform handles the unsync automatically.,")
        Question(id: "56", question: "An Order has been created from an activated Contract and its status changed to \"Activated.\" A rep tries to edit the Order Product quantities directly. What happens?", options: [("A", "Editing is restricted once an Order is Activated; specific fields become locked to preserve the fulfillment record's integrity"), ("B", "The edit succeeds freely with no restriction"), ("C", "The Order automatically reverts to Draft status to allow the edit"), ("D", "Only the Contract, not the Order, can ever be edited after activation")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Once an Order moves to Activated status, editing restrictions kick in on key fields like Order Product quantities, since an activated order represents a committed fulfillment record that shouldn't be casually altered. * B is incorrect: Unrestricted editing after activation would undermine the purpose of the Draft → Activated lifecycle control. C is incorrect: There's no automatic revert-to-Draft behavior triggered by an edit attempt on an activated Order. D is incorrect: The described restriction applies to the Order's own activated state, not exclusively to the Contract.,")
        Question(id: "57", question: "An Account Team member is added to an Account with the \"Account Read Only\" team role, on an Account whose OWD is Private. Which TWO statements about this member's resulting access are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The team member automatically gets full access to related Opportunities and Cases regardless of configured settings"), ("B", "Account Teams never grant access to related records, only to the Account itself"), ("C", "Access to related Opportunities and Cases depends on the specific Opportunity Access and Case Access levels configured for that team member"), ("D", "Account Teams let the admin/owner configure separate Account, Opportunity, and Case access levels per team member"), ("E", "Manually adding the team member to each individual Opportunity and Case is required in addition to Account Team settings")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "* Account Teams expose separately configurable Account, Opportunity, and Case access levels per member, and it's precisely those explicit settings — not a blanket default — that determine what related-record access the team member actually receives. * A: Access isn't unconditionally automatic — it's explicitly configured per the Opportunity/Case Access fields on the team member. * B: Account Teams can indeed extend access to related Opportunities and Cases when configured to do so. * E: Manual record-by-record addition isn't required when Account Team access settings already extend that access.,")
        Question(id: "58", question: "How does Opportunity Team sharing differ from Account Team sharing in terms of what it tracks?", options: [("A", "Opportunity Teams and Account Teams are functionally identical with no differences"), ("B", "Opportunity Teams support member-specific roles and revenue split percentages tied to that specific deal, while Account Teams are broader and persist across all the account's opportunities by default"), ("C", "Account Teams support split percentages; Opportunity Teams do not"), ("D", "Opportunity Teams can only include users who are also on the Account Team")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Opportunity Teams are deal-specific, supporting roles and revenue split percentages unique to that Opportunity, whereas Account Teams apply account-wide and don't carry deal-specific split tracking. * A is incorrect: The two mechanisms serve different scopes and carry different data (splits vs. account-wide roles), so they aren't identical. C is incorrect: Split percentages are an Opportunity Team feature, not an Account Team one — this option reverses the actual capability. D is incorrect: Opportunity Team membership isn't restricted to only users already on the Account Team.,")
        Question(id: "59", question: "A sales director wants to adjust a subordinate's Q3 forecast total upward to reflect deals expected to close, without changing the subordinate's individually displayed number. What forecasting feature supports this?", options: [("A", "Editing the subordinate's Opportunity Amount fields directly"), ("B", "Changing the subordinate's forecast category manually on each Opportunity"), ("C", "Forecast adjustments, which let a manager override the rolled-up number their subordinate contributes without modifying the subordinate's own forecast page"), ("D", "Reassigning the subordinate's opportunities to the director temporarily")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Forecast adjustments are specifically designed to let a manager change the rolled-up number they see for a subordinate in the forecast hierarchy, without altering what the subordinate sees on their own forecast. * A is incorrect: Directly editing Opportunity Amounts changes the underlying deal data, not just a forecast-level adjustment, and affects the rep's own view too. B is incorrect: Manually changing forecast categories on individual opportunities is a blunt, record-level workaround, not the adjustment feature designed for this. D is incorrect: Reassigning ownership changes actual deal ownership and reporting, which is a much larger and inappropriate change for a forecast adjustment need.,")
        Question(id: "60", question: "An Opportunity is in a custom stage called \"Verbal Commitment\" that the admin created. Sales leadership wants this stage's pipeline dollars to show up in the \"Commit\" forecast category. What must be configured?", options: [("A", "Rename the stage to \"Commit\""), ("B", "Create a new forecast type specifically for this stage"), ("C", "Forecast categories cannot be customized for custom stages"), ("D", "Map the custom stage to the Commit forecast category in the Opportunity Stage settings")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Every Opportunity Stage, including custom ones, maps to one of the standard forecast categories (Pipeline, Best Case, Commit, Closed, Omitted), and that mapping is configured directly in the stage's settings. * A is incorrect: Renaming the stage doesn't change its underlying forecast category mapping — that's a separate, explicit setting. B is incorrect: A whole new forecast type is unnecessary overhead for what's a simple stage-to-category mapping change. C is incorrect: Forecast category mapping is fully configurable for any stage, custom or standard.,")
        Question(id: "61", question: "A territory model is Active, and an opportunity is manually associated with Territory West. The sales operations team wants that opportunity's amount to roll into Territory West's forecast rather than the owner's individual forecast alone. What enables this?", options: [("A", "Territory forecasting, which allows opportunities associated with a territory to roll into that territory's forecast hierarchy"), ("B", "Changing the Opportunity owner to a Territory-named user"), ("C", "A custom roll-up summary field on the Territory object"), ("D", "Manually recreating the deal as a new record under the territory")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Territory forecasting is designed exactly for this — opportunities tied to an active territory can roll their amounts into that territory's forecast hierarchy, separate from or alongside individual owner forecasts. * B is incorrect: Reassigning ownership to a fictitious \"territory user\" is not how territory forecasting works and would corrupt ownership data. C is incorrect: Territory forecasting is a native forecasting hierarchy feature, not something built from a custom roll-up field. D is incorrect: Duplicating the deal record is unnecessary and would create duplicate pipeline data.,")
        Question(id: "62", question: "A knowledge manager needs to ensure a newly published KB article about a security vulnerability is visible only to internal support agents, not to customers browsing the public help center. What should be configured?", options: [("A", "Set the article's Record Type to \"Internal Only\""), ("B", "Assign the article to a Data Category visible only to the internal-facing category group, excluding the customer-facing group"), ("C", "Set the article's Article Type field to \"Private\""), ("D", "Remove the article from all data categories entirely")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Data category group visibility is the mechanism that controls which audience (internal users, partners, or customers) can see a given article, so scoping the article's category group to internal-only achieves the required restriction. * A is incorrect: Record Type controls layout and fields, not audience-based visibility restrictions on publication. C is incorrect: There's no \"Private\" Article Type field value that functions as an audience visibility toggle. D is incorrect: Removing all data categories doesn't guarantee restriction — category group visibility settings are the actual control, and an uncategorized article's visibility behavior isn't the intended fix here.,")
        Question(id: "63", question: "A published Knowledge article is edited by an author to fix an error. What happens to the live article customers are currently viewing while the edit is in progress?", options: [("A", "The article is immediately taken offline until the edit is completed and republished"), ("B", "Customers see the edit in real time as the author types"), ("C", "The published version remains live and unaffected while a separate draft version is created for the edit"), ("D", "The published version is overwritten immediately upon the first keystroke")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Salesforce Knowledge's versioning model creates a new draft version when a published article is edited, leaving the currently published version live and visible until the new draft is itself published. * A is incorrect: There's no forced offline period during editing — the published version continues serving customers throughout. B is incorrect: Edits happen in the draft version and aren't reflected live until published; customers don't see in-progress changes. D is incorrect: The live published version isn't touched until the draft replaces it via publishing, not on the first edit action.,")
        Question(id: "64", question: "A support org sets up an Entitlement Process with milestones for First Response and Resolution. A case is created but the Entitlement lookup field on the Case is blank. What likely setting was missed?", options: [("A", "The Case Status field was left blank"), ("B", "The Case Origin field wasn't set to \"Web\""), ("C", "The support agent forgot to run a validation rule manually"), ("D", "\"Set Default Entitlement\" wasn't configured, so the case's Entitlement lookup wasn't auto-populated based on the related Account or Contact")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* The \"Set Default Entitlement\" setting governs whether a case's Entitlement field auto-populates by matching against the related Account or Contact's entitlements — without it configured, the field stays blank on new cases. * A is incorrect: Case Status has no direct bearing on Entitlement lookup auto-population. B is incorrect: Case Origin doesn't control entitlement matching logic. C is incorrect: There's no manual validation rule step involved in entitlement auto-population; it's a platform setting, not a rule the agent triggers.,")
        Question(id: "65", question: "An Entitlement Process has a First Response milestone with a 4-hour completion window, linked to a Business Hours record of Monday–Friday, 9am–5pm. A case comes in Friday at 4pm. Which TWO statements about the milestone timing are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The violation time is Friday at 8pm, counting calendar hours continuously"), ("B", "The timer is paused indefinitely once outside business hours and never resumes"), ("C", "The violation time lands at Monday 12pm, since only business hours count toward the countdown"), ("D", "Linking the process to a Business Hours record is what causes non-business hours to be excluded from the countdown"), ("E", "The timer pauses over the weekend and resumes counting Monday morning rather than continuing over non-business hours")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 4], explanation: "* Because the entitlement process is tied to Business Hours, the countdown pauses outside 9–5 M–F and resumes Monday morning, so the remaining time from Friday 4pm (1 hour left before the weekend) completes at Monday 12pm. * A: Calendar-hour counting ignores the Business Hours linkage, which specifically restricts counting to defined working hours. * B: The timer isn't paused forever — it resumes counting when business hours restart, it just doesn't advance while outside them. * D: While true as a general fact, it doesn't specify the actual resulting violation time the way C and E do together, making it a less complete answer for this pair.,")
        Question(id: "66", question: "A service manager wants agents to see a custom \"Escalate to Tier 2\" button directly in the Case Feed publisher, alongside the default Log a Call and Send Email actions. What should be configured?", options: [("A", "Add a custom Quick Action to the Case Feed's publisher actions"), ("B", "Rebuild the Case Feed entirely with Visualforce"), ("C", "Add the action only to the Case list view"), ("D", "Use a validation rule to force escalation before save")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Case Feed publisher actions are extensible with custom Quick Actions, which is exactly how a custom \"Escalate to Tier 2\" button gets added alongside the default actions like Log a Call and Send Email. * B is incorrect: Rebuilding the entire feed in Visualforce is a drastic, unnecessary approach when native Quick Action extensibility already covers this. C is incorrect: A list view button doesn't place the action inside the Case Feed publisher where agents work case-by-case. D is incorrect: A validation rule enforces data integrity on save; it doesn't add an actionable UI button to the feed.,")
        Question(id: "67", question: "A company's public-facing support form submits inquiries directly into Salesforce as Cases via Web-to-Case. On a high-traffic day, 6,200 submissions come in. What happens?", options: [("A", "All 6,200 cases are created successfully with no daily limit applied"), ("B", "Only the first 5,000 are created that day; excess submissions during that period are not converted into cases via Web-to-Case"), ("C", "The extra 1,200 are automatically queued and created the next day"), ("D", "Web-to-Case throttles to exactly 1,000 per hour regardless of daily volume")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Web-to-Case enforces a 5,000 case-per-day creation limit, so submissions beyond that daily cap during the same 24-hour period are not converted into case records. * A is incorrect: This ignores the documented daily cap that does apply to Web-to-Case submissions. C is incorrect: There's no automatic next-day queuing/rollover mechanism for submissions that exceed the daily limit. D is incorrect: The limit is a daily cap, not a flat hourly throttle of 1,000.,")
        Question(id: "68", question: "A support org wants inbound customer emails to automatically become Cases without requiring any firewall configuration or dedicated email server setup on their end. Which feature should be used?", options: [("A", "Standard Email-to-Case, requiring firewall-routed email forwarding"), ("B", "Web-to-Case exclusively"), ("C", "On-Demand Email-to-Case, using a Salesforce-generated email address"), ("D", "A custom Apex email service class built from scratch")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* On-Demand Email-to-Case uses a Salesforce-generated email address that customers or forwarding rules send to directly, avoiding any need for the company to configure firewall routing or a dedicated email relay. * A is incorrect: Standard Email-to-Case does require firewall-based routing configuration, which is exactly what this scenario wants to avoid. B is incorrect: Web-to-Case handles form submissions, not inbound email conversion. D is incorrect: A custom Apex email service is unnecessary engineering effort when the native On-Demand option already meets the requirement.,")
        Question(id: "69", question: "A contact center wants work routed to the agent with the best-matching skill set for each incoming chat, rather than simply the next available agent in a queue. Which Omni-Channel routing model fits?", options: [("A", "Queue-based routing"), ("B", "External routing"), ("C", "Round-robin routing with no skill matching"), ("D", "Skill-based routing")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Skill-based routing matches incoming work items to agents whose configured skills align with the specific need, rather than distributing purely by queue position or agent availability order. * A is incorrect: Queue-based routing pushes work by priority and capacity within a queue, without skill-matching logic. B is incorrect: External routing hands off routing decisions to an external system, which isn't what's being asked for here. C is incorrect: Plain round-robin without skill consideration is the opposite of the skill-matching requirement described.,")
        Question(id: "70", question: "An agent's Omni-Channel presence status is configured with a capacity limit of 3 chats and 1 case. The agent is currently at 3 active chats and 1 case. A new chat request comes in. What happens?", options: [("A", "The new chat is routed to a different available agent, since this agent's capacity is fully consumed across configured channels"), ("B", "The chat is force-assigned anyway, exceeding the configured capacity"), ("C", "The agent's case work is automatically paused to make room"), ("D", "The presence status automatically changes to Offline")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Presence configuration capacity limits are enforced per channel type, so once an agent hits their configured capacity across chats and cases, new work routes to another available agent instead of overloading this one. * B is incorrect: Omni-Channel respects configured capacity limits and does not force-assign beyond them. C is incorrect: There's no automatic pausing of existing case work to free capacity for new chat requests. D is incorrect: Reaching capacity doesn't automatically flip the presence status to Offline; the agent remains available for channels within their remaining capacity.,")
        Question(id: "71", question: "A queue set up for chat routing in Omni-Channel has no agents currently available (all are at capacity or offline). What happens to an incoming chat routed to that queue?", options: [("A", "The chat is automatically rejected and the customer receives an error"), ("B", "The chat waits in queue until an agent becomes available, based on the routing configuration"), ("C", "The chat is routed to a completely unrelated queue automatically"), ("D", "The chat converts into an email case automatically")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* When no agents are available, Omni-Channel work items wait in the queue according to the configured routing and priority rules, ready to be pushed to the next agent who becomes available. * A is incorrect: There's no automatic customer-facing rejection error triggered simply because agents are momentarily unavailable. C is incorrect: Work items don't automatically reroute to an unrelated queue without explicit configuration to do so. D is incorrect: Automatic conversion to an email case isn't a native behavior of queued, unavailable-agent chat routing.,")
        Question(id: "72", question: "A partner organization needs its users to log cases, view opportunities, and collaborate on deals registered to them, with more object access than a pure self-service customer portal user. Which Experience Cloud license type fits best?", options: [("A", "Customer Community license"), ("B", "Guest User access only"), ("C", "Partner Community license"), ("D", "Standard internal Salesforce license")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Partner Community licenses are built for external partner users needing broader CRM object access (like Opportunities for deal registration) beyond the narrower self-service scope of a standard Customer Community license. * A is incorrect: Customer Community licenses are scoped for consumer self-service use cases like case logging, with more limited object access than partners typically need for deal collaboration. B is incorrect: Guest User access is unauthenticated and far too limited for a logged-in partner needing to view and act on Opportunities. D is incorrect: A full internal Salesforce license is unnecessary and inappropriate for an external partner organization's users.,")
        Question(id: "73", question: "A company wants to allow anonymous website visitors to browse public Knowledge articles on an Experience Cloud site without logging in, but restrict case creation to authenticated users only. What access level should the anonymous browsing experience use?", options: [("A", "Authenticated Customer access for everyone, including anonymous visitors"), ("B", "Partner Community access for anonymous visitors"), ("C", "Internal user access temporarily granted to anonymous sessions"), ("D", "Guest User access, with case creation object permissions withheld from the Guest User profile")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Guest User access is exactly the unauthenticated browsing tier, and simply not granting Case object create permissions on the Guest User profile keeps case creation restricted to users who log in. * A is incorrect: Authenticated Customer access requires login, which contradicts the requirement for anonymous browsing. B is incorrect: Partner Community access is for authenticated partner users, not anonymous public visitors. C is incorrect: Granting internal access to anonymous sessions would be a severe over-grant of access and isn't how Experience Cloud guest access works.,")
        Question(id: "74", question: "An ISV's managed package includes an Apex trigger that subscribers cannot view or modify the code for, but subscribers can still configure custom settings exposed by the package. What packaging characteristic explains this?", options: [("A", "Managed packages hide Apex code behind the package namespace while still exposing configurable custom settings/metadata the ISV chooses to expose"), ("B", "This behavior only happens with unmanaged packages"), ("C", "Subscribers always have full code visibility regardless of package type"), ("D", "Custom settings cannot be included in any package type")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Managed packages specifically hide the underlying Apex code from subscribers while still allowing the ISV to expose certain configurable elements, like custom settings, for subscriber-side configuration. * B is incorrect: Unmanaged packages expose all code openly, the opposite of the hidden-code behavior described. C is incorrect: Full code visibility for subscribers is the unmanaged package behavior, not the managed one seen here. D is incorrect: Custom settings absolutely can be included and exposed within managed packages for subscriber configuration.,")
        Question(id: "75", question: "A company wants to surface read-only data from an external ERP system inside a Salesforce report, refreshed live from the source without any Salesforce storage. Using Salesforce Connect external objects, what limitation should the admin expect regarding reporting?", options: [("A", "External objects support full native reporting with no limitations whatsoever"), ("B", "External objects generally cannot be used directly in standard Salesforce reports the way native or custom objects can"), ("C", "External objects can only be reported on through Objects and Applications reports"), ("D", "External objects automatically convert into custom report types")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* A known limitation of Salesforce Connect external objects is that they generally aren't reportable through standard Salesforce reporting the way native/custom objects are, which is an important constraint to plan around. * A is incorrect: This directly contradicts the well-documented reporting limitation on external objects. C is incorrect: There's no such thing as an \"Objects and Applications report\" category that carves out an exception for external objects. D is incorrect: External objects don't automatically convert into a different reportable structure; the limitation persists as-is.,")
        Question(id: "76", question: "A sales operations team wants Account Teams applied automatically to every new Enterprise-segment Account created, using a consistent set of team members and roles. What feature supports this default setup?", options: [("A", "A validation rule that blocks account creation without a team"), ("B", "A required custom field capturing team member names as text"), ("C", "Default Account Team templates, applied automatically based on the user's settings when they create qualifying accounts"), ("D", "Manual entry of team members on every new account")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Default Account Team functionality lets a user configure a standard team template that automatically applies itself to new accounts they create, ensuring consistent membership and roles without manual re-entry each time. * A is incorrect: A validation rule blocking creation without a team doesn't add the desired team members automatically; it only errors out data entry. B is incorrect: A text field capturing names doesn't create genuine Account Team sharing/access at all. D is incorrect: Manual entry on every account is the repetitive process the default team template is specifically designed to eliminate.,")
        Question(id: "77", question: "A sales VP wants product-family-level forecasting in addition to standard revenue forecasting, so leadership can see pipeline broken out by product line. What must be true to enable this?", options: [("A", "Product Family forecasting must be enabled as an additional forecast type, alongside the org's existing revenue/quantity forecasting configuration"), ("B", "This requires deleting the existing revenue forecasting type entirely"), ("C", "Product Family forecasting is only available if Person Accounts are enabled"), ("D", "It requires a custom Apex batch job to calculate family-level totals nightly")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Product Family forecasting is a distinct, natively supported forecast type that can be enabled in addition to revenue or quantity forecasting, giving leadership the product-line breakdown without replacing existing forecast types. * B is incorrect: Enabling product family forecasting doesn't require removing the existing revenue forecast type; multiple forecast types can coexist. C is incorrect: Person Accounts enablement is unrelated to whether Product Family forecasting is available. D is incorrect: This is a native, declarative forecasting type — no custom Apex batch calculation is required. --- #,")
        Question(id: "78", question: "An admin needs a custom field to store agent notes up to 10,000 characters, searchable in SOQL WHERE clauses with partial-text matching. Which field type is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "Long Text Area"), ("B", "Text"), ("C", "Rich Text Area"), ("D", "Text Area")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Long Text Area supports up to 131,072 characters (well beyond the 10,000 needed) and, unlike Rich Text Area, its plain-text content can be filtered in SOQL WHERE clauses using LIKE, satisfying both the length and query requirements. * B is incorrect: A standard Text field caps out at 255 characters, far short of the 10,000-character requirement. C is incorrect: Rich Text Area stores HTML formatting and is not reliably filterable in SOQL WHERE clauses the way plain long text is. D is incorrect: A basic Text Area field caps out at 255 characters, the same ceiling as standard Text.,")
        Question(id: "79", question: "A data steward configures a Duplicate Rule with a Matching Rule comparing Email fields, set to Block, scoped Lead-to-Lead only. A user tries to save a new Lead with an email matching an existing Contact exactly. Which TWO statements are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The save is always blocked regardless of scope, since Block always applies org-wide across all objects"), ("B", "The Lead is automatically converted into the matching Contact"), ("C", "The matching rule automatically expands scope to include Contacts once a business email domain is detected"), ("D", "The save succeeds, because a Lead-to-Lead scoped matching rule won't catch a match against a Contact record"), ("E", "A cross-object matching rule would need to be explicitly configured to catch this Lead-to-Contact match")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4], explanation: "* Matching/duplicate rule scope determines exactly which object pairs get compared — a Lead-to-Lead scoped rule won't catch a Contact match, and catching it would require explicitly building a cross-object matching rule instead. * A: Block action only fires when the configured matching rule scope actually detects a match; scope, not the Block action itself, determines whether Contacts are even compared. * B: There's no automatic Lead-to-Contact conversion triggered simply by a duplicate match being found. * C: Matching rule scope doesn't dynamically expand itself based on email domain patterns.,")
        Question(id: "80", question: "A company wants cross-object duplicate detection so that new Leads are checked against existing Contacts, not just other Leads, using email as the match key. What must be configured?", options: [("A", "A cross-object matching rule comparing Lead.Email to Contact.Email, referenced by a duplicate rule scoped across both objects"), ("B", "A validation rule on Lead referencing the Contact object"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field showing Contact counts on Lead"), ("D", "This isn't possible; matching rules can only compare an object against itself")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Matching rules support cross-object configuration, letting a field like Email be compared between Lead and Contact, and a duplicate rule can then be scoped to check across both objects for genuine cross-object duplicate detection. * B is incorrect: A validation rule enforces data entry logic on save; it isn't the mechanism for duplicate detection against another object. C is incorrect: A roll-up summary field aggregates child records under a master-detail relationship; it doesn't perform duplicate matching logic. D is incorrect: Cross-object matching rules are explicitly supported and are exactly the tool for this requirement.,")
        Question(id: "81", question: "An admin needs a report showing total Opportunity Amount subtotaled by Sales Rep, and separately by Product Family, in the same report with grouping and subtotals — but no need to compare across separate primary objects. Which report format fits?", options: [("A", "Summary report, with two grouping levels"), ("B", "Tabular report"), ("C", "Joined report"), ("D", "Matrix report with only row groupings and no column groupings")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* A Summary report supports multiple grouping levels (like Sales Rep and Product Family) with subtotals at each level, which is exactly what's needed without requiring the cross-object block comparison that Joined reports are built for. * B is incorrect: Tabular reports offer no grouping or subtotal capability at all. C is incorrect: Joined reports are overkill and intended for combining different primary objects into separate blocks, not simple multi-level grouping within one dataset. D is incorrect: A Matrix report without column groupings underuses its two-dimensional design; a Summary report is the more natural fit for this single-dimension multi-level grouping need.,")
        Question(id: "82", question: "A user tries to build a Joined report with 6 different report blocks, each showing a different object's data. What happens?", options: [("A", "All 6 blocks save successfully with no limitation"), ("B", "The 6th block is automatically merged into the 5th"), ("C", "Joined reports have no block limit, only a row limit"), ("D", "The save fails, because Joined reports support a maximum of 5 blocks")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Joined reports are capped at 5 blocks maximum, so attempting to add a 6th block will be blocked until the count is brought back within the limit. * A is incorrect: This exceeds the documented 5-block cap that Joined reports enforce. B is incorrect: There's no automatic merging behavior for a block beyond the limit; the add action itself is restricted. C is incorrect: The 5-block limit is a real, enforced constraint, not a myth to be dismissed in favor of only a row limit.,")
        Question(id: "83", question: "A company needs a report combining fields from Opportunity, its primary Contact Role, Account, and Campaign — four objects deep — and no standard report type covers this combination. Which TWO statements about the Custom Report Type needed are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A Custom Report Type can chain a primary object plus up to three additional related objects, covering all four objects here"), ("B", "Custom Report Types can only ever have one related object, never a chain"), ("C", "The \"with\" vs. \"with or without\" relationship setting controls whether parentless/childless records appear in the results"), ("D", "The primary object must be a custom object, never a standard object like Opportunity"), ("E", "All related objects in a Custom Report Type must share the same OWD setting")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "* Custom Report Types support a relationship chain of up to four total objects (one primary plus three related), and the \"with\" vs. \"with or without\" setting is exactly what governs whether parent records lacking a related child still appear. * B: Custom Report Types explicitly support relationship chains beyond a single related object, up to four objects total. * D: The primary object can be standard or custom; there's no restriction requiring it to be custom. * E: OWD settings on related objects have no bearing on whether they can be chained together in a Custom Report Type.,")
        Question(id: "84", question: "A report needs to group numeric Deal Size values into \"Small,\" \"Medium,\" and \"Large\" buckets for a quick visual breakdown, without building a new formula field. The bucketed groups will also be referenced in a cross-report formula on a dashboard. Is this achievable as described?", options: [("A", "Bucket fields can group the values as needed, but they cannot be referenced in cross-report formulas — that's a documented limitation"), ("B", "Bucket fields fully support being referenced directly in cross-report formulas"), ("C", "Bucketing requires a custom field to be created first"), ("D", "Bucket fields only work with picklist and text fields, never numeric fields")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Bucket fields are great for ad hoc grouping of numeric, picklist, or text values without a new field, but a known limitation is that bucket fields cannot be used inside cross-report formulas, which matters for the dashboard requirement described. * B is incorrect: This directly contradicts the documented cross-report formula limitation on bucket fields. C is incorrect: The entire benefit of bucket fields is avoiding the need to build a new custom/formula field first. D is incorrect: Bucket fields work with numeric fields as well as picklist and text fields, contrary to this claim.,")
        Question(id: "85", question: "A sales manager wants a report showing all Accounts that have never had a single Opportunity created, without writing a custom formula. What declarative reporting feature enables this?", options: [("A", "A bucket field on Account"), ("B", "A cross filter using \"Accounts without Opportunities\""), ("C", "A joined report combining Accounts and Opportunities"), ("D", "A roll-up summary field showing zero Opportunities")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Cross filters with a \"Without\" condition are specifically designed to surface parent records lacking any matching child records, which is exactly the case of Accounts with zero related Opportunities. * A is incorrect: Bucket fields group existing field values; they don't test for the absence of related child records. C is incorrect: A joined report combines separate blocks side by side but doesn't inherently filter for the absence of child records without a cross filter. D is incorrect: A roll-up summary field on a lookup relationship (Account to Opportunity) isn't natively supported the way it is for master-detail, and doesn't itself function as a filtering mechanism.,")
        Question(id: "86", question: "A regional VP wants a summary report where each region's total is expressed as a percentage of the overall company total, calculated within the report itself using a value from a higher grouping level. Which function should be used in a Custom Summary Formula?", options: [("A", "PREVGROUPVAL, referencing the previous peer group"), ("B", "A simple SUM formula field"), ("C", "PARENTGROUPVAL, referencing the grand total or a higher-level grouping"), ("D", "A cross-object formula field on Opportunity")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* PARENTGROUPVAL pulls a value from a higher grouping level (such as the grand total) into a calculation at a lower grouping level, which is exactly what's needed to express each region's total as a percentage of the whole. * A is incorrect: PREVGROUPVAL references the previous peer group at the same level (useful for period-over-period comparisons), not a higher-level total. B is incorrect: A simple SUM field can't reference a different grouping level's total within the same report. D is incorrect: A cross-object formula field lives on a single record, not across report grouping levels, and can't perform this kind of report-level percentage calculation.,")
        Question(id: "87", question: "A dashboard needs to display data as seen by each individual viewer's own record access, rather than a single fixed running user. The org already has 5 dynamic dashboards and the license allows only 5. Which TWO statements are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Dynamic dashboards are capped by a license-based limit on the total number allowed in the org"), ("B", "Standard dashboards can also be configured to run as each individual viewer, just like dynamic dashboards"), ("C", "There is no limit on the number of dynamic dashboards an org can have"), ("D", "To add a 6th dynamic dashboard, the admin must either free an existing slot or acquire additional dynamic dashboard capacity"), ("E", "A report-level filter can be used to bypass the dynamic dashboard licensing limit")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "* Dynamic dashboards are capped by a license-enforced limit, and exceeding that ceiling (here, 5) requires either freeing an existing dynamic dashboard slot or acquiring more capacity before a new one can be created. * B: Standard dashboards always run as a single specified running user, not as each individual viewer, so this claim is false. * C: This ignores the real, license-enforced limit on the number of dynamic dashboards an org can have. * E: Report-level filters don't address dashboard viewing-user behavior or the dynamic dashboard licensing limit at all.,")
        Question(id: "88", question: "A dashboard currently has 3 filters applied. Sales operations wants to add a 4th filter so users can also slice by Product Family. What happens?", options: [("A", "The 4th filter is added with no issue"), ("B", "Adding a 4th filter automatically removes the oldest one"), ("C", "Dashboard filters have no maximum; only components have limits"), ("D", "The 4th filter cannot be added; dashboards support a maximum of 3 filters")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Dashboards are capped at 3 filters maximum, so a 4th cannot be added until one of the existing three is removed or repurposed. * A is incorrect: This exceeds the documented 3-filter maximum per dashboard. B is incorrect: There's no automatic replacement of an existing filter; the platform simply blocks adding a 4th. C is incorrect: The 3-filter cap is a real, enforced dashboard-level limit, contrary to this claim.,")
        Question(id: "89", question: "A VP wants to track how the count of Open Cases has trended week over week for the past year, beyond what standard historical trending (3 months) supports. What declarative solution fits?", options: [("A", "A Reporting Snapshot, scheduling a summary/matrix report to run regularly and storing the results in a custom object for long-term historical trend reporting"), ("B", "Historical Trending, since it already supports a full year of data"), ("C", "A single roll-up summary field on Case"), ("D", "A Big Object without any scheduled batch process")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Reporting Snapshots let you schedule a summary or matrix report's results to write into a custom object on a recurring basis, building up exactly the kind of long-term historical trend data that native Historical Trending's 3-month window doesn't support. * B is incorrect: Historical Trending is limited to roughly the past 3 months, well short of the full year needed here. C is incorrect: A roll-up summary field gives a single current aggregate, not a time-series trend across many past snapshots. D is incorrect: A Big Object without a scheduled process to actually populate it wouldn't capture any snapshot data on its own.,")
        Question(id: "90", question: "Historical Trending is enabled on Opportunity, tracking 6 fields. The admin tries to add 3 more tracked fields, for a total of 9. What happens, given the platform's field limit for this feature?", options: [("A", "The addition fails, since Historical Trending supports a maximum of 8 tracked fields on supported objects like Opportunity, and 9 exceeds that cap"), ("B", "The addition fails outright since only 6 fields are ever allowed"), ("C", "All fields beyond the first 3 are silently ignored"), ("D", "Historical Trending has no field limit at all")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Historical Trending supports tracking up to 8 fields on supported objects like Opportunity. Going from 6 to 9 tracked fields exceeds that 8-field cap, so the addition fails. * B is incorrect: The field limit for Historical Trending is 8, not 6, so this understates the actual allowed maximum — the failure happens for a different reason than this option claims. C is incorrect: There's no silent partial-ignore behavior; the platform enforces the field cap directly and blocks the addition outright. D is incorrect: Historical Trending does enforce a specific field limit (8), contrary to this claim.,")
        Question(id: "91", question: "A company needs an off-platform backup solution meeting a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of less than 24 hours for its Salesforce data. Which native/complementary approach best satisfies this?", options: [("A", "The native Weekly Export Service alone"), ("B", "Relying solely on Field History Tracking"), ("C", "A third-party continuous or frequent backup solution, since the native Weekly Export Service's cadence doesn't meet a sub-24-hour RPO"), ("D", "Relying solely on Setup Audit Trail")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* The native Weekly Export Service runs on a much longer cadence than 24 hours, so meeting a sub-24-hour RPO requires a more frequent backup approach, typically a third-party or scheduled Data Loader-based solution running far more often. * A is incorrect: Weekly Export's cadence is measured in days/weeks, nowhere close to satisfying a sub-24-hour RPO. B is incorrect: Field History Tracking records field-level changes, but it isn't a comprehensive backup/restore mechanism for full data recovery. D is incorrect: Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration changes, not a data backup and recovery mechanism at all.,")
        Question(id: "92", question: "A company wants to archive 10 years of closed Case records for compliance retention, keeping them queryable but out of the primary Case object to preserve performance, with no need for triggers or flows on the archived data. What should be used?", options: [("A", "A custom object identical to Case with Apex triggers migrating records nightly"), ("B", "Field History Tracking configured on Case"), ("C", "A Full Copy sandbox dedicated to archived data"), ("D", "A Big Object, with appropriate Index fields configured to support querying")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Big Objects are purpose-built for large-scale data archiving, support SOQL-like querying (via configured Index fields), and explicitly don't support triggers or flows — which matches both the archiving need and the stated lack of automation requirement. * A is incorrect: A regular custom object with triggers reintroduces automation overhead the scenario explicitly says isn't needed and adds ongoing performance/maintenance burden. B is incorrect: Field History Tracking records changes to existing records; it isn't an archival storage mechanism for entire closed records. C is incorrect: A sandbox is a testing/development environment, not a durable production archive strategy for compliance retention.,")
        Question(id: "93", question: "An admin needs to import 150,000 new Contact records with upsert logic matching against an existing External_ID__c field, without writing code. Which TWO statements are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Data Loader supports upsert operations at a volume well beyond Data Import Wizard's capacity"), ("B", "Data Import Wizard supports unlimited record volume and upsert just as well as Data Loader"), ("C", "External IDs aren't usable by data tools like Data Loader for matching purposes"), ("D", "Manual entry through the Contact list view is the recommended approach for this volume"), ("E", "External ID fields let Data Loader match incoming records to existing ones without needing the Salesforce record ID")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 4], explanation: "* Data Loader is the tool suited to this volume of upsert operations, and it's exactly the External ID field that lets it match incoming records to existing Salesforce records without needing to already know the record ID. * B: Data Import Wizard is capped at a much lower record count and isn't suited to a 150,000-record load. * C: External ID fields are explicitly designed to be used by data tools like Data Loader for upsert matching, contrary to this claim. * D: Manual entry for 150,000 records is wildly impractical and not a genuine tool-based solution.,")
        Question(id: "94", question: "A finance team needs Opportunity data surfaced from an external billing system in near real time inside Salesforce, but governor limits on External Object queries are a known concern for a report showing thousands of rows. What should the admin communicate about this approach?", options: [("A", "Salesforce Connect external objects are subject to governor limits when queried, and large-scale reporting against them may hit callout or row limits that a native object wouldn't encounter"), ("B", "External objects have no governor limits whatsoever since data isn't stored in Salesforce"), ("C", "Governor limits only apply to Apex, never to declarative queries against external objects"), ("D", "External objects automatically batch and cache all results with no limit concerns")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Because Salesforce Connect external objects rely on live callouts to the external system, they are subject to relevant governor limits (like callout limits) that can constrain large-scale queries, an important caveat to flag for a large reporting use case. * B is incorrect: Not storing data in Salesforce doesn't exempt external object queries from governor limit considerations tied to the underlying callouts. C is incorrect: Governor limits on callouts and query rows apply broadly, not solely within Apex execution contexts. D is incorrect: There's no unlimited automatic caching behavior that eliminates governor limit exposure for external object queries.,")
        Question(id: "95", question: "A nonprofit needs to know, for GDPR-style compliance requests, exactly which fields on a Contact record changed over the last year and by whom. Fields being tracked include Email, Phone, and MailingAddress — all under the 20-field Field History Tracking cap. What is the correct approach and its limitation?", options: [("A", "Use Setup Audit Trail exclusively, since it tracks field-level record data changes"), ("B", "Enable Field History Tracking on those fields; the UI retains 18 months of history, and older or larger-scale queries should go through the API rather than the UI"), ("C", "Use Login History exclusively, since it tracks all user activity including field edits"), ("D", "This requires Shield Platform Encryption to be enabled first")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Field History Tracking is exactly the tool for tracking specific field-level changes and who made them, with an 18-month UI retention window, meaning anything beyond that (or bulk querying needs) should be handled via the API against the history data. * A is incorrect: Setup Audit Trail tracks configuration/metadata changes (like profile or setup changes), not individual record field value changes. C is incorrect: Login History tracks authentication events only, not field-level data edits on records. D is incorrect: Shield Platform Encryption is unrelated to whether Field History Tracking can be enabled and used. --- #,")
        Question(id: "96", question: "A company needs a sandbox for a 3-month UAT cycle testing against a realistic subset of production data, refreshed roughly every 5 days as new production data flows in. Which sandbox type fits, and what should the admin control the data subset with?", options: [("A", "Full Copy sandbox, refreshed daily"), ("B", "Developer sandbox, since UAT doesn't need real data"), ("C", "Partial Copy sandbox, using a sandbox template to define which objects' data is included"), ("D", "Developer Pro sandbox with no template needed")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Partial Copy sandboxes support a 5-day minimum refresh cycle and use a sandbox template to define exactly which objects' sample data gets copied, matching both the refresh cadence and realistic-subset requirement described. * A is incorrect: Full Copy's 29-day minimum refresh interval doesn't match the roughly 5-day cadence needed here, and it copies all data rather than a defined subset. B is incorrect: Developer sandboxes contain no production data, which conflicts with the UAT need for realistic sample data. D is incorrect: Developer Pro sandboxes don't use sandbox templates for production data sampling the way Partial Copy does.,")
        Question(id: "97", question: "An admin refreshes a Partial Copy sandbox that had several custom flows manually deactivated for testing purposes before the refresh. After the refresh completes, what is true about those flows?", options: [("A", "The flows remain deactivated exactly as they were pre-refresh"), ("B", "All flows in the sandbox are deleted after any refresh"), ("C", "Flow activation status is preserved only if Field History Tracking is enabled"), ("D", "The flows revert to whatever active/inactive state they have in production, since the refresh pulls a fresh metadata copy from production")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* A sandbox refresh pulls a new copy of both data and metadata from production, so any sandbox-specific customization like deactivating a flow is overwritten and the flow reverts to its current production activation state — which is exactly why admins must remember to deactivate automation again post-refresh if needed. * A is incorrect: Sandbox-specific state changes made before a refresh don't survive the refresh; they get overwritten by the production copy. B is incorrect: A refresh doesn't delete flows outright; it replaces the sandbox's metadata with a fresh copy from production, which still contains the flows. C is incorrect: Field History Tracking is unrelated to whether flow activation status persists through a sandbox refresh.,")
        Question(id: "98", question: "Two orgs need a one-time metadata migration for a small set of custom fields and validation rules. Both admins have System Administrator access in their respective orgs. What must be established before a change set can be sent from the source org to the target?", options: [("A", "A deployment connection must be authorized between the two orgs, established by a System Administrator in both orgs"), ("B", "Both orgs must be merged into a single org first"), ("C", "The metadata must first be packaged as a managed package"), ("D", "No connection is needed; change sets deploy automatically between any two orgs")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Change sets require an explicit, unidirectional deployment connection authorized by System Administrators in both the source and target orgs before any outbound change set can be sent and deployed. * B is incorrect: Merging orgs is an extreme and unnecessary step; change sets work between separate, connected orgs. C is incorrect: Managed packaging is an AppExchange distribution mechanism, not a prerequisite for a simple change set migration between two orgs an admin controls. D is incorrect: Change sets absolutely require an established, authorized connection — they don't deploy automatically between arbitrary orgs.,")
        Question(id: "99", question: "An admin validates an outbound change set in the target sandbox, and the validation completes successfully with all tests run within the last 10 days. What deployment option becomes available, and what does it require?", options: [("A", "Quick Deploy is always available regardless of validation history"), ("B", "Quick Deploy, which requires a change set that was already validated (with all tests run) within the last 10 days"), ("C", "Rollback Deploy, allowing the admin to undo the change set at any time"), ("D", "Auto Deploy, which skips validation checks entirely going forward")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Quick Deploy becomes available specifically when a change set has already been validated with all its tests successfully run within the prior 10 days, letting the actual deployment skip re-running that validation step. * A is incorrect: Quick Deploy isn't unconditionally available; it depends on a recent, successful validation matching the 10-day window. C is incorrect: There's no native \"Rollback Deploy\" feature for change sets; change sets cannot automatically roll back a completed deployment. D is incorrect: There's no \"Auto Deploy\" option that permanently skips validation; each deployment still depends on its own validation state.,")
        Question(id: "100", question: "A change set includes a profile with several permission changes, alongside a permission set with additional permissions. Which TWO statements about deployment behavior are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Both the profile and permission set deploy their full definitions completely"), ("B", "The profile's permissions in the change set deploy incrementally, applying only what's explicitly included"), ("C", "The permission set deploys its complete definition in full"), ("D", "Neither the profile nor the permission set changes deploy via change sets"), ("E", "Only the permission set deploys; profiles can never be included in change sets")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "* Profiles in a change set apply only the specific permissions explicitly captured (incremental, not a full overwrite), while permission sets included in the same change set deploy their entire definition in full. * A: Profiles specifically do not deploy their full definition through a change set — only the included permissions apply, unlike permission sets. * D: Both profiles and permission sets can be included in change sets and do deploy, just with different behaviors. * E: Profiles absolutely can be included in change sets; this option is factually incorrect.,")
        Question(id: "101", question: "An admin wants to migrate a customized set of Opportunity Stage picklist values from a sandbox to production using a change set. Which TWO statements are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Change sets fully support migrating all picklist value changes with no exceptions"), ("B", "Opportunity Stage values cannot be included in change sets"), ("C", "Picklist values can only ever be migrated via Data Loader, never any other method"), ("D", "Lead Source picklist values are similarly excluded from change sets and must be migrated manually"), ("E", "The values migrate automatically the first time any change set is deployed")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "* Both Opportunity Stage values and Lead Source picklist values are explicitly among the metadata types excluded from change sets and must be manually recreated or migrated in the target org. * A: This directly contradicts the documented exclusion of certain metadata types like these from change sets. * C: Data Loader handles data records, not picklist value definitions/metadata, so it isn't the right tool for this either. * E: There's no automatic migration of excluded metadata types simply by deploying an unrelated change set.,")
        Question(id: "102", question: "A Developer sandbox was refreshed yesterday. The team now wants to refresh it again today because of an urgent need for the latest production data. What are they told?", options: [("A", "They must wait a full 29 days regardless of sandbox type"), ("B", "Developer sandboxes can never be refreshed more than once per month"), ("C", "Developer and Developer Pro sandboxes have a 1-day minimum refresh interval, so refreshing again today should generally be possible, unlike Partial Copy (5 days) or Full Copy (29 days)"), ("D", "Refresh intervals only apply to Full Copy sandboxes")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Developer and Developer Pro sandboxes carry the shortest refresh interval restriction at just 1 day, so an admin can generally refresh again the next day, in contrast to the much longer intervals for Partial Copy and Full Copy. * A is incorrect: The 29-day interval applies specifically to Full Copy sandboxes, not Developer sandboxes. B is incorrect: This misstates the actual 1-day minimum interval that applies to Developer sandboxes. D is incorrect: Refresh interval restrictions apply across all sandbox types, just at different minimum durations.,")
        Question(id: "103", question: "A team wants to avoid a full sandbox refresh cycle but still needs a near-instant new sandbox environment that mirrors an existing sandbox's current metadata and data state. What alternative should they consider?", options: [("A", "A brand-new Full Copy sandbox request from production, ignoring the existing sandbox"), ("B", "Change sets deployed into a newly created empty Developer sandbox"), ("C", "Data Loader replication into a new Developer Pro sandbox"), ("D", "Sandbox cloning, which creates a new sandbox instance based on an existing sandbox's current state without waiting through a full production refresh cycle")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Sandbox cloning is designed exactly for this need — spinning up a new sandbox based on an existing sandbox's current state quickly, without going through a full production-based refresh cycle. * A is incorrect: Requesting fresh from production ignores the existing sandbox's current customized state, which the team specifically wants mirrored. B is incorrect: Change sets migrate metadata piecemeal into an empty sandbox and wouldn't replicate the full data and metadata state as directly as cloning. C is incorrect: Data Loader handles record data migration, not a full metadata-plus-data sandbox state replication. --- #,")
        Question(id: "104", question: "An admin needs a field on Opportunity auto-calculated and saved in the same transaction as the record save, without consuming a separate DML statement. Which automation tool fits, and why?", options: [("A", "A before-save Record-Triggered Flow, since it updates the triggering record's own fields without a separate DML operation"), ("B", "An after-save Record-Triggered Flow, since it always runs first"), ("C", "A Schedule-Triggered Flow running nightly"), ("D", "A Process Builder process, since it's the modern standard")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* A before-save flow modifies fields on the very record being saved, and those changes get folded into the same underlying DML operation already happening for the save, avoiding any extra DML statement. * B is incorrect: After-save flows run following the initial DML commit and require their own separate DML operation to make further updates, consuming governor limit resources unnecessarily for a same-record field calculation. C is incorrect: A nightly schedule wouldn't calculate the field at save time, missing the real-time requirement. D is incorrect: Process Builder is retired for new automation and isn't the modern recommended tool regardless.,")
        Question(id: "105", question: "A recurring reminder task needs to fire every Monday at 8am for all open Opportunities closing that week, rather than reacting to a specific record's save event. Which tool is the better fit compared to a scheduled path on a record-triggered flow?", options: [("A", "A before-save Record-Triggered Flow"), ("B", "A Schedule-Triggered Flow, since it runs independent of any individual record save event and can process a broad, time-based batch of records"), ("C", "An approval process"), ("D", "A validation rule with a time-based condition")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* A Schedule-Triggered Flow is designed for exactly this kind of independent, calendar-based batch processing that isn't tied to any specific record's save event, unlike a scheduled path which still originates from a triggering record update. * A is incorrect: Before-save flows only fire on a record's own save event, not as an independent weekly batch process. C is incorrect: Approval processes handle step-by-step human review workflows, not scheduled batch reminders. D is incorrect: Validation rules only fire on record save to block/allow data entry; they can't run independently on a schedule.,")
        Question(id: "106", question: "A multi-step approval process needs the final rejection to revert the Opportunity's Stage field back to its pre-submission value automatically. Where should this field update be configured?", options: [("A", "As an initial submission action"), ("B", "As a step-level approval action only"), ("C", "As a final rejection action on the approval process"), ("D", "Approval processes cannot update fields on rejection")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Final rejection actions on an approval process are exactly where field updates meant to fire once a record is fully rejected (like reverting Stage) should be configured, distinct from actions tied to submission or intermediate approval steps. * A is incorrect: Initial submission actions fire when the record first enters the approval process, not when it's ultimately rejected. B is incorrect: Step-level approval actions apply to a specific approval step being approved, not to the overall final rejection outcome. D is incorrect: Approval processes explicitly support field updates as final rejection actions, contrary to this claim.,")
        Question(id: "107", question: "A five-step approval process needs Step 3 to route to whichever user is specified in a lookup field on the record, which varies by record. Which TWO statements about approver types are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Current user's manager routes based on a lookup field value rather than the submitter's role hierarchy position"), ("B", "The related user (field-based lookup) approver type resolves dynamically to whichever user is populated in the specified lookup field"), ("C", "A designated approver hardcoded per step would automatically vary based on the record's lookup field"), ("D", "Related user is the only approver type among these that resolves differently per record based on record data"), ("E", "A queue approver type routes to any member of a group rather than to one specific record-driven individual")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "* The related user approver type is exactly the one that resolves per-record based on a lookup field, while queue-based routing is fundamentally different, spreading approval across any member of a defined group rather than one specific person. * A: Current user's manager routes based on the submitter's role hierarchy position, not an arbitrary lookup field value, so this reverses the actual behavior. * C: A hardcoded designated approver is fixed and would not vary per record the way a field-based lookup does. * D: While true in isolation, it doesn't identify a second correct configuration behavior the way B does, making it a weaker second selection than B for this pairing.,")
        Question(id: "108", question: "A finance team needs a record to be completely locked from editing by anyone except a delegated approver while it moves through a multi-step, reassignable human review process. Which automation tool is the only one that provides this record-locking behavior?", options: [("A", "A Record-Triggered Flow with a Fault path"), ("B", "A validation rule"), ("C", "A Screen Flow"), ("D", "An Approval Process")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Approval Processes are the one declarative tool that can lock a record during multi-step review, restricting edits to admins and delegated approvers, which no other declarative automation tool natively provides. * A is incorrect: Flows don't have a native record-locking mechanic tied to a human review workflow the way approval processes do. B is incorrect: Validation rules block invalid saves but don't lock a record from all editing during a review process. C is incorrect: Screen Flows collect user input interactively but don't provide record-locking during a multi-step approval journey.,")
        Question(id: "109", question: "A validation rule checks that Amount > 0 on Opportunity. A before-save flow on the same object sets Amount based on a calculation involving other fields. In what order do these two mechanisms evaluate, and why does that matter for resolving a conflict?", options: [("A", "The before-save flow's field changes are visible to the validation rule since validation rules re-evaluate after before-save flow updates but before the record is committed via DML"), ("B", "The validation rule always fires completely before the before-save flow runs, so the flow's Amount calculation is invisible to it"), ("C", "Both run simultaneously with no defined order"), ("D", "The validation rule only evaluates after the record has already committed to the database")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* In the order of execution, before-save flows run and modify field values, and system validation (including custom validation rules) evaluates against the resulting field state before that single DML operation commits — meaning the validation rule sees the flow-calculated Amount. * B is incorrect: This misstates the order — before-save flows update field values that validation rules do evaluate against, not the reverse where the rule is blind to those changes. C is incorrect: There's a defined, deterministic order of execution, not simultaneous evaluation with no sequence. D is incorrect: Validation rules fire before the DML commit, not after — the whole point is blocking invalid data before it's saved.,")
        Question(id: "110", question: "A legacy business process still relies on a Process Builder process built years ago to send an email when a Case is closed. The admin wants to add new logic triggered on the same event. What should the admin do?", options: [("A", "Add the new logic directly inside the existing Process Builder process, since Process Builder remains the recommended tool for all new automation"), ("B", "Build the new logic in a Record-Triggered Flow, since Process Builder is retired for new automation, and plan an eventual migration of the old process's logic into Flow as well"), ("C", "Build the new logic as a Workflow Rule, since it's the newest automation tool available"), ("D", "Use only Apex triggers going forward, since declarative tools are no longer supported")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Process Builder is retired for new automation, so new logic should be built in Flow (the modern replacement), while existing legacy processes continue to run until they're migrated. * A is incorrect: Building new logic inside a retired tool contradicts Salesforce's guidance to use Flow for all new automation going forward. C is incorrect: Workflow Rules are an older tool than Process Builder, not newer, and are also not the recommended path for new automation. D is incorrect: Declarative tools like Flow remain fully supported and are the recommended approach; Apex isn't required for this kind of email-on-close logic.,")
        Question(id: "111", question: "A Record-Triggered Flow debug run needs to simulate execution against a specific set of input values without actually committing those changes to the database. What Flow Builder capability supports this?", options: [("A", "Cloning the flow into a sandbox and running it live against real records"), ("B", "Setting the flow's status to Draft, which prevents any DML"), ("C", "Debug mode, which lets the admin set field values for the run and does not commit resulting DML to the database"), ("D", "There is no way to simulate a flow run without committing changes")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Flow Builder's Debug feature lets you simulate a run with specified input values and inspect the execution path and resulting values without those changes being committed to the actual database. * A is incorrect: Running live against real records in a sandbox still commits actual DML in that environment, rather than simulating without commitment. B is incorrect: Draft status prevents the flow from being triggered by real events, but it isn't the mechanism for a controlled, inspectable debug simulation. D is incorrect: Debug mode explicitly provides this simulation capability, contrary to this claim.,")
        Question(id: "112", question: "An after-save Record-Triggered Flow on Opportunity fails during execution for several records over a weekend. What does the admin see, and how should they find and act on the failures?", options: [("A", "Salesforce sends flow error emails (if configured) and the admin can review and resume/delete entries in the Paused and Failed Flow Interviews list"), ("B", "The flow silently retries indefinitely with no visibility to the admin"), ("C", "The records are automatically rolled back to their pre-flow state permanently, with no error notification"), ("D", "Failed flow runs are only visible by contacting Salesforce Support"), ("E", "There is no fault-related monitoring available for flows in the platform")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Salesforce sends flow error emails detailing the failure (element, error message, flow version, running user) and failed flow interviews land in the Paused and Failed Flow Interviews list, where an admin can review, resume, or delete them. * B is incorrect: Flows don't silently retry indefinitely without any admin-visible trace; failures are surfaced through error emails and the failed interviews list. C is incorrect: A failed flow doesn't automatically and silently roll back with zero notification — error emails are the standard notification path. D is incorrect: Admins can review failed flow interviews directly in Setup without needing a support case. E is incorrect: The platform does provide native fault-related monitoring for flows via error emails and the Paused and Failed Flow Interviews list, contrary to this claim.,")
        Question(id: "113", question: "An after-save flow updates a field on the same Opportunity record it just fired on, which then re-triggers the same flow, risking an infinite loop. What Flow Builder mechanism should the admin use to prevent this?", options: [("A", "A decision element checking isChanged() or comparing against $Record__Prior to only proceed if the relevant field actually changed in a meaningful way"), ("B", "Deactivating the flow entirely, disabling the automation"), ("C", "Converting the flow to a Screen Flow so it requires manual triggering"), ("D", "Adding a Wait element for 24 hours before the update runs")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Comparing current values to $Record__Prior (or using isChanged()) lets the flow detect whether the field genuinely changed since the prior execution, letting it skip redundant updates and break the recursive triggering cycle. * B is incorrect: Deactivating the flow removes the automation entirely, which doesn't solve the underlying recursion problem if the logic is still needed. C is incorrect: Converting to a Screen Flow removes automatic triggering altogether, which isn't a scoped recursion-prevention technique and changes the automation's fundamental behavior. D is incorrect: A Wait element delays execution but doesn't address or prevent the recursive re-triggering itself.,")
        Question(id: "114", question: "Two after-save Record-Triggered Flows exist on the same object and the same trigger event (record update). What determines the order in which they execute relative to each other?", options: [("A", "They always execute in the order they were created, with no way to change it"), ("B", "They execute in alphabetical order by flow label within that trigger context, unless explicitly reordered via trigger order settings"), ("C", "Execution order is random each time"), ("D", "Only one after-save flow can ever exist per object")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* Multiple record-triggered flows sharing the same object and trigger event execute in a defined order (by default alphabetical by label), and this order can be explicitly controlled using the flow trigger order setting. * A is incorrect: Creation order isn't what determines execution sequence, and the order is in fact configurable via flow trigger order settings. C is incorrect: Execution order is deterministic and controllable, not random. D is incorrect: Multiple after-save flows can coexist on the same object and trigger event; there's no such one-flow restriction.,")
        Question(id: "115", question: "A flow performs a Get Records call inside a loop, once per iteration, to check a related record's status for 500 iterating records. Which TWO statements about this design are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Flows automatically bulkify any Get Records call regardless of where it's placed, including inside loops"), ("B", "The fix is to add a Wait element inside the loop between each Get Records call"), ("C", "This risks hitting SOQL query governor limits, since one query fires per loop iteration"), ("D", "The fix is to move the Get Records call outside the loop using a collection-based bulk query instead"), ("E", "Converting the flow into a Screen Flow resolves the underlying governor limit issue")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "* Issuing a Get Records call inside a loop runs one query per iteration, quickly consuming the per-transaction SOQL query limit at scale, and the fix is to move that query outside the loop as a single bulk query against a collection. * A: Automatic bulkification applies to properly structured Get/Create/Update/Delete elements outside loops — placing the call inside the loop defeats that built-in efficiency. * B: A Wait element adds delay but does nothing to reduce the actual number of SOQL queries being issued. * E: Converting to a Screen Flow changes the trigger model entirely and doesn't address the underlying bulkification issue.,")
        Question(id: "116", question: "A Record-Triggered Flow processes a large batch update of 3,000 records in a single transaction, with a loop iterating over related child records for each parent. Which TWO statements about governor limits here are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Loops have no iteration limit as long as Get/Create/Update/Delete elements are bulkified"), ("B", "The loop iteration limit is dynamically tied to the size of the specific triggering batch"), ("C", "Flow enforces a maximum of 2,000 loop iterations within a single transaction"), ("D", "The loop iteration limit is 50,000, matching the total records-queried limit per transaction"), ("E", "Exceeding the loop iteration limit causes the flow to throw an error, regardless of how well other elements are bulkified")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 4], explanation: "* Flow enforces a hard, fixed cap of 2,000 loop iterations per transaction, and exceeding that cap throws an error regardless of whether other elements in the flow are properly bulkified. * A: Bulkifying Get/Create/Update/Delete elements doesn't remove the separate, hard 2,000-iteration loop limit. * B: The loop iteration limit is a fixed platform number, not dynamically tied to the specific triggering batch size. * D: 50,000 is a different platform limit (like total records queried) and isn't the loop iteration cap.,")
        Question(id: "117", question: "An admin wants to build a reusable block of logic (checking a customer's tier and calculating a discount) that can be called from multiple different parent Autolaunched Flows across the org, including potentially from a before-save context. Can a subflow be used here?", options: [("A", "Yes, subflows can be called from any flow context including before-save flows with no restriction"), ("B", "No, subflows can never be called from another flow"), ("C", "Yes for most contexts, but subflows cannot be called from a before-save record-triggered flow context"), ("D", "Subflows can only be used within Screen Flows")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* Subflows are the right tool for reusable logic called from other flows, but a known constraint is that subflows cannot be invoked from a before-save record-triggered flow context, which matters for exactly the scenario described. * A is incorrect: This ignores the specific before-save-context restriction on subflow invocation. B is incorrect: Subflows are explicitly designed to be called from other flows; this option is simply false. D is incorrect: Subflows aren't restricted to Screen Flow contexts — they're broadly reusable across most autolaunched flow contexts.,")
        Question(id: "118", question: "A company has both an active Lead Assignment Rule and wants to add a second active Lead Assignment Rule to handle a new region's routing logic simultaneously. What happens?", options: [("A", "Both can be active simultaneously and Salesforce merges their logic automatically"), ("B", "The most recently activated rule silently overrides the older one without needing to be edited"), ("C", "Assignment rules have no limit on how many can be simultaneously active"), ("D", "Only one Lead Assignment Rule can be active at a time; the new logic must be added as additional entries within the single active rule")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "* Only one Lead (or Case) Assignment Rule can be active at any given time, so new regional routing logic must be added as additional rule entries within that same single active rule rather than as a separate second active rule. * A is incorrect: There's no automatic merging behavior between two active assignment rules — only one can be active regardless. B is incorrect: While activating a new rule does deactivate the old one, describing it as \"silently overriding without needing to be edited\" misrepresents that the new rule must be built with its own complete entries, not just added on top. C is incorrect: The single-active-rule limitation is a real, enforced platform constraint per object.,")
        Question(id: "119", question: "A case escalation rule is configured to escalate any case not resolved within 4 business hours. A case is created, and 3 hours later an agent updates an unrelated field on it. Does the escalation timer reset because of that update?", options: [("A", "No, escalation rules evaluate cases on a scheduled basis independent of the save transaction, and they do not re-fire or reset based on an unrelated field update after the threshold was already set"), ("B", "Yes, any update to the case automatically restarts the 4-hour escalation window"), ("C", "The escalation rule pauses entirely once any field is edited"), ("D", "Escalation rules only evaluate Case Status changes, never time thresholds")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "* Case escalation rules run as a time-based, scheduled evaluation separate from the record save transaction, and they don't reset or re-fire simply because an unrelated field was updated after the escalation threshold was already established. * B is incorrect: An unrelated field update doesn't restart the escalation clock; escalation timing is independent of ad hoc field edits. C is incorrect: There's no pause-on-edit behavior for escalation rules tied to arbitrary field changes. D is incorrect: Escalation rules are fundamentally about time thresholds, not exclusively about Status field changes.,")
        Question(id: "120", question: "An org has both a flow Email Alert action and an approval process email alert configured, both firing frequently for a high-volume standard-license user. Which TWO statements about the shared email constraint are correct? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Flow email alerts and approval process email alerts draw from entirely separate, independent daily limits"), ("B", "There is no daily email limit for automation-triggered emails, only for manually sent emails"), ("C", "The daily limit only applies to Workflow Rule email alerts, not Flow or approval process emails"), ("D", "Flow Email Alert actions and approval process email alerts draw from the same daily email limit tied to the user license"), ("E", "Workflow Rule email alerts also draw from that same shared daily email limit, not a separate one")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4], explanation: "* Flow Email Alert actions, approval process email alerts, and Workflow Rule email alerts all draw from the same underlying daily email limit tied to the user license (plus a separate org-wide daily cap), so heavy automation across any of these mechanisms for the same user can collectively hit that shared ceiling. * A: The limits aren't separate per mechanism — they share the same underlying daily email allotment. * B: Automation-triggered emails are indeed subject to the same daily limits as other outbound email from the org. * C: The daily limit applies across Workflow Rules, Flow email alerts, and approval process alerts alike, not just to Workflow Rules.,")
        Question(id: "121", question: "A business process needs cross-object rollups from a grandchild object through a lookup relationship (not master-detail), with complex conditional aggregation logic, real-time low-latency requirements, and heavy recursive control needs beyond what a flow loop can cleanly express. What should the admin recommend?", options: [("A", "A roll-up summary field, since it supports lookup relationships identically to master-detail"), ("B", "Apex code (e.g., a trigger or invocable class), since this combination of requirements exceeds what declarative automation tools can cleanly deliver"), ("C", "A single before-save flow, since flows can always meet any complexity requirement"), ("D", "A Workflow Rule with a cross-object field update")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "* When a requirement combines lookup-relationship rollups (which roll-up summary fields don't support), complex conditional aggregation, tight latency needs, and recursion control beyond flow loop capabilities, Apex is the appropriate escalation point beyond declarative tools. * A is incorrect: Roll-up summary fields require a master-detail relationship; they do not work on lookup relationships the way this scenario requires. C is incorrect: Flows have real governor limits and expressive constraints (like the 2,000-iteration loop cap) that make them unsuitable for every arbitrarily complex requirement. D is incorrect: Workflow Rules cannot perform cross-object rollup aggregation logic like this, and they're also retired for new automation.,")
        Question(id: "122", question: "An approval process's entry criteria is set to \"Evaluate the rule when a record is created, and every time it's edited.\" A record that already went through and completed the approval process is edited again afterward, with no relevant fields changed from the approval's perspective. What happens?", options: [("A", "The record can never re-enter an approval process once completed, regardless of entry criteria settings"), ("B", "Entry criteria settings only affect initial submission, never subsequent edits"), ("C", "Depending on entry criteria configuration, the record could re-enter the approval process on any edit; using \"evaluate only when specified fields change\" is the correct way to prevent unnecessary re-submission on unrelated edits"), ("D", "Approval processes automatically ignore all edits after the first completion by design")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "* With criteria set to evaluate on every edit, even unrelated field changes can trigger re-evaluation and potential re-entry into the approval process; configuring entry criteria to evaluate only when specific relevant fields change is the correct way to avoid unwanted re-submission. * A is incorrect: Whether a completed record can re-enter approval depends entirely on how entry criteria are configured, not a blanket permanent block. B is incorrect: Entry criteria settings directly govern behavior on subsequent edits, which is exactly the mechanism at play in this scenario. D is incorrect: There's no automatic universal exclusion of post-completion edits; that behavior must be deliberately configured via entry criteria. ---")
    ]
